	// target-specific number. Two ops, each naming an INTENT, leaves each backend to spell that intent in
	// its own vocabulary.
	osFileOpenWriteExecutable(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}
	// --- The WHOLE environment, which `osEnvRead` cannot reach ---
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE VERY END OF THE UNION, past `osFileOpenWriteExecutable`, per the band-append
	// invariant every entry above states: a mid-band insert is silently swallowed by a `… to X` range arm.
	// Both carry `category: system`, placing them logically in the system band independent of this tail
	// position.
	//
	// `osEnvBlock(result)` = a pointer to this process's whole environment IN THE PLATFORM'S OWN SHAPE, or 0
	// when the host could not answer. `osCommandLine`'s shape exactly, and for its reason: the API hands back
	// a pointer rather than writing through one.
	//   • x64-windows — `GetEnvironmentStringsA()`: a COPY, NUL-terminated `NAME=VALUE` entries back-to-back
	//     with one more NUL after the last.
	//   • arm64-macOS — `*_NSGetEnviron()`: the LIVE `environ`, a NULL-terminated vector of `char *`.
	// `osEnvBlockFree(blockPtr)` = `FreeEnvironmentStringsA(blockPtr)` — the release a COPY owes, emitted only
	// by the shape that takes one.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OP IS THE QUESTION, NOT THE ANSWER'S LAYOUT**, and `SubprocessRuntime.EnvironmentSnapshotShape`
	// is where the layout is decided — once, in the one producer, so no consumer has to ask what target it is
	// running on. That separation is what makes a single op pair honest across two genuinely different data
	// structures.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **`osEnvRead` CANNOT BE ASKED THIS QUESTION, WHICH IS WHY THERE IS A SECOND ENVIRONMENT OP.** It
	// answers ONE NAME the caller already knows. Building a child's environment means walking the names the
	// caller does NOT know — that is what `Environment.inheritUpdating` means — and no sequence of
	// `GetEnvironmentVariableA` calls can enumerate them.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO OPS AND NOT ONE, because a Win32 snapshot is OWNED.** `GetEnvironmentStringsA` returns a copy
	// the caller must release, so a single "read the environment" op would either leak one block per spawn or
	// have to hide the free inside a lowering that cannot know when the walk is finished.
	//
	// ⭐ **GATED ON `HostFacility.subprocess`, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME SET AS "x64-windows".** The sole caller
	// is `SubprocessRuntime.buildSubpEnvEntry`, whose entry point is under the `__gt_subp_` prefix
	// `SemanticCheck.calleeHostFacility` maps to that facility; `targetProvidesFacility` answers `true` for
	// x64-windows AND arm64-macOS, so BOTH lower `osEnvBlock` and only the first ever sees `osEnvBlockFree`.
	// A backend the facility denies never sees either, which is why wasm stubs neither — a stub would answer
	// "this process has no environment", and a child built from that answer would start with an EMPTY one.
	// Full call-barrier metadata: both ARE indirect calls once lowered.
	osEnvBlock(resultId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: false, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}
	osEnvBlockFree(blockPtrId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}
	// --- The ORDERED memory band (G3) — the one publication a second OS thread reads ---
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE VERY END OF THE UNION, past `osEnvBlockFree`, per the band-append invariant every
	// entry above states: a mid-band insert is silently swallowed by a `… to X` range arm. Both carry
	// `category: memory`, placing them logically beside `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` independent of this
	// tail position.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **`storeRelease` / `loadAcquire` ARE `storeIndirect` / `loadIndirect` PLUS AN ORDERING
	// OBLIGATION, AND NOTHING ELSE.** Same four fields, same widths, same field displacement. What they add
	// is the half-barrier a publication needs: **a release store orders every EARLIER memory access before
	// itself, and an acquire load orders every LATER one after itself.** A writer that publishes with
	// `storeRelease` and a reader that observes with `loadAcquire` therefore agree on everything the writer
	// wrote before publishing — which is the entire property `gt.result` / `gt.threw` depend on.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THEY EXIST BECAUSE `StdOp.atomicCas`'s NEIGHBOURING NOTE PREDICTED THE EXACT SHAPE THAT WOULD
	// NEED THEM AND IT ARRIVED.** That note licenses no fence for the Treiber stack (*"on arm64 the CAS
	// publishes with `STLXR`, a store-RELEASE"*) and names the shape to watch for: *"a store-load pair with
	// no CAS between them"*. A green thread's `completed` publication IS that pair — three plain stores and
	// a plain load, with no atomic anywhere between them — and it stopped being latent when the scheduler
	// grew genuinely concurrent worker Ms on two weakly-ordered lanes (`pthread_create` on arm64-macOS,
	// `clone` on arm64-linux). See `GtRuntime.emitGtStoreStatus` for the publication and its checker.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AN ORDERED FLAVOUR RATHER THAN A STANDALONE FENCE OP, AND THE REASON IS THE ISA.** A fence would
	// be `DMB ISH` on arm64 — a FULL barrier ordering every access in both directions — where `STLR`/`LDAR`
	// order exactly the half each side needs, in one instruction, with no separate op to sit at the wrong
	// side of a branch. It is also the shape that costs x64 **nothing**: x86-64 is TSO, so an ordinary `mov`
	// already IS a release store and an acquire load, and both lower to the identical instruction the plain
	// ops lower to — byte-for-byte the same emitted code, where a fence op would have had to decide between
	// emitting a needless `mfence` and emitting nothing at all under a name that says otherwise.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FIELD DISPLACEMENT IS KEPT, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES x64 AND wasm FREE.** `atomicCas`/`atomicRmw`
	// carry no offset — an atomic names one word — and every caller of theirs computes an address first. Had
	// these copied that, x64 would have paid an extra `lea`/`add` at every ordered access for an offset its
	// addressing mode carries anyway. Only arm64 pays: `STLR`/`LDAR` are base-register-only forms with no
	// offset field at all, so its lowering folds `base + offset` into the IP scratch exactly as
	// `StdToArm64Conversion.addressFor` already does for an out-of-band displacement.
	//
	// Metadata is `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect`'s, field for field, and for their reasons — `isPure: false`
	// above all, which is what forbids a pass from moving, duplicating or dropping one. `clobbersFlags:
	// false` is exact on both lanes: `mov`, `STLR` and `LDAR` write no condition flags.
	loadAcquire(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueType StdType) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.memory, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: false, isMemory: true, isStore: false, isCall: false, clobbersFlags: false, isCmp: false}
	storeRelease(addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueId ValueId, valueType StdType) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.memory, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: false, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: false, clobbersFlags: false, isCmp: false}
	// --- WHAT KIND OF OBJECT IS THIS STANDARD-STREAM HANDLE? ---
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE VERY END OF THE UNION, past `storeRelease`, per the band-append invariant every
	// entry above states: a mid-band insert is silently swallowed by a `… to X` range arm. It carries
	// `category: system`, placing it logically in the system band independent of this tail position.
	//
	// `osHandleFileType(result, handle)` = `GetFileType(handle)`. One VALUE operand — the handle
	// `osStdHandle` answers with — and it DEFINES the file-type word. `FILE_TYPE_CHAR` (2) is the answer
	// that means "a character device", which is what `__tty_stdout_wants_color` tests for.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT ANSWERS WIN32'S OWN ENUMERATION AND NOT A BOOL, WHICH IS THIS TIER'S ESTABLISHED
	// CONVENTION RATHER THAN A CONCESSION.** `osFileAttributes` already hands `GetFileAttributesA`'s DWORD
	// to a target-neutral runtime builder that masks it and tests `FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY`
	// (`ManagedDirectoryRuntime.buildManagedDirectoryExists`), and the POSIX lane translates its `stat`
	// into that vocabulary rather than the Std tier learning a second one
	// (`Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s errno→Win32 mapping makes the identical trade). A lane with `isatty` answers
	// `FILE_TYPE_CHAR` when it is true and `FILE_TYPE_DISK` when it is not, which is the same translation
	// one question over.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE RAW WORD IS WHY THE CALLER MASKS.** `GetFileType` answers a DWORD, so the upper half of
	// the register it arrives in is whatever the callee left there — a raw i64 compared against 2 would
	// answer `false` for a genuine console the moment that half was not zero. `__md_exists` records the
	// same hazard from the other end (a register holding `0x00000000FFFFFFFF` is not -1), and the cure is
	// the same one: mask with `DwordMask` in the builder, where it is one Std op and cannot be forgotten
	// per lane.
	//
	// ⚠ **`isStore: false`** — the call writes through no pointer the caller owns, which is `osEnvBlock`'s
	// position rather than `osEnvRead`'s. Every other call-barrier flag is set: it IS an indirect call once
	// lowered.
	osHandleFileType(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: false, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}
end 'StdOp'

export typealias StdOpArray = Array with StdOp

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH ARITHMETIC SHAPE AN OP CARRIES — ONE ROSTER, READ BY ALL THREE ARITHMETIC PASSES.**
// `foldConstOperands` (move a const OPERAND into an immediate), `foldConstants` (EVALUATE
// `const <op> const`) and `eliminateCommonSubexpressions` (reuse a value the block already computed)
// each ask "what does this op compute, out of what?" of the same union. Each used to open — or would
// have opened — with its own exhaustive `StdOp` match whose every other arm answered "unchanged": the
// same set, enumerated three times, in three files that would learn about a new variant separately.
// This is that enumeration, once.
//
// ⚠ **THE SHAPE SET GREW FOR `EC13` AND THE TWO FOLD PASSES ARE UNAFFECTED BY THE GROWTH**, because
// each asks a `shape` PREDICATE (`isBinaryArith`/`isComparison`) rather than reading `shape` directly.
// `unaryOp`, `binOpImm` and `cmpImm` used to answer `recordNeither` here with their reasons stated at
// the arms; those reasons were about what a CONST FOLD can do with them, not about what they compute,
// so each now records its real shape and the fold passes' two predicates still answer `false` for it.
//
// ⚠ **`div`/`mod` STAY OUT, AND THAT IS THE ONE EXCLUSION THAT IS ABOUT SEMANTICS RATHER THAN ABOUT A
// PASS.** They are `isPure: false` — x64 `idiv` faults on a zero divisor and on `i64.min / -1` — so no
// fold may evaluate one away and no CSE may drop the second of two. Admitting them here would put a
// trapping op in front of three passes that all treat this roster's answer as a licence to rewrite.
// `eliminateCommonSubexpressions` additionally re-asks `op.rawValue.isPure` of every shape this roster
// admits and PANICS on a disagreement, so the two statements cannot drift apart.
//
// A caller-owned RECORD, never a returned union, for `StdFoldableDef`'s measured reason: a
// payload-bearing union heap-boxes EVERY case including the payload-free ones, and this sits on a
// path walked once per op in the module.
export enum StdArithShape
	// No arithmetic at all: a terminator, a call, a memory access, an address, an OS primitive, or one
	// of the two trapping divisions. Nothing any arithmetic pass acts on.
	neither
	// A `binOp` — `lhs`, `rhs`, `opcode` and `operandType` are meaningful.
	binaryArith
	// A `cmp` — `lhs`, `rhs`, `pred` and `operandType` are meaningful.
	comparison
	// A `unaryOp` — `lhs` (the one operand), `unaryOpcode` and `operandType` are meaningful.
	unary
	// A `binOpImm` — `lhs`, `imm`, `opcode` and `operandType` are meaningful.
	binaryArithImm
	// A `cmpImm` — `lhs`, `imm`, `pred` and `operandType` are meaningful.
	comparisonImm
end 'StdArithShape'

export type StdArithOperands
	// ⚠ **THE FIELDS BELOW ARE MEANINGLESS UNTIL `shape` SAYS WHICH OF THEM THE LAST classification
	// FILLED**, and none may be read as a sentinel — ValueId 0 is a real value, `StdBinOpcode.add` is a
	// real opcode and `StdCmpPred.eq` a real predicate. Every reader asks a `shape` predicate first.
	var shape as StdArithShape
	export var resultId as ValueId
	// The FIRST value operand, and the only one a `unary` or an immediate form has.
	export var lhs as ValueId
	// The SECOND value operand — `binaryArith` and `comparison` only (`hasSecondValueOperand`).
	export var rhs as ValueId
	// `binaryArith` and `binaryArithImm`.
	export var opcode as StdBinOpcode
	// `comparison` and `comparisonImm`.
	export var pred as StdCmpPred
	// `unary` only.
	export var unaryOpcode as StdUnaryOpcode
	// The two IMMEDIATE forms only — an ENCODED LITERAL, not a value operand, which is why
	// `remapOperandsThrough` leaves it alone and `computesSameValueAs` compares it directly.
	export var imm as Imm32
	// Set by EVERY shape: it is the type the operation READS, and it is what tells a pass whether this
	// is float arithmetic (which the two fold passes may not touch — see each pass's own float guard).
	export var operandType as StdType

	export static function create() returns StdArithOperands
		return Self{shape: StdArithShape.neither, resultId: 0, lhs: 0, rhs: 0, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, unaryOpcode: StdUnaryOpcode.neg, imm: 0, operandType: StdType.i64}
	end 'create'

	function recordNeither()
		self.shape = StdArithShape.neither
	end 'recordNeither'

	// The parameters are named for the ROLE rather than for the field, because a local that shadows a
	// `self` field is E3006 — see the diagnostic's own wording on why the compiler refuses it.
	function recordBinOp(defined ValueId, left ValueId, right ValueId, binOpcode StdBinOpcode, readType StdType)
		self.shape = StdArithShape.binaryArith
		self.resultId = defined
		self.lhs = left
		self.rhs = right
		self.opcode = binOpcode
		self.operandType = readType
	end 'recordBinOp'

	function recordCmp(defined ValueId, left ValueId, right ValueId, cmpPred StdCmpPred, readType StdType)
		self.shape = StdArithShape.comparison
		self.resultId = defined
		self.lhs = left
		self.rhs = right
		self.pred = cmpPred
		self.operandType = readType
	end 'recordCmp'

	function recordUnaryOp(defined ValueId, only ValueId, unaryOpcodeRead StdUnaryOpcode, readType StdType)
		self.shape = StdArithShape.unary
		self.resultId = defined
		self.lhs = only
		self.unaryOpcode = unaryOpcodeRead
		self.operandType = readType
	end 'recordUnaryOp'

	function recordBinOpImm(defined ValueId, left ValueId, immediate Imm32, binOpcode StdBinOpcode, readType StdType)
		self.shape = StdArithShape.binaryArithImm
		self.resultId = defined
		self.lhs = left
		self.imm = immediate
		self.opcode = binOpcode
		self.operandType = readType
	end 'recordBinOpImm'

	function recordCmpImm(defined ValueId, left ValueId, immediate Imm32, cmpPred StdCmpPred, readType StdType)
		self.shape = StdArithShape.comparisonImm
		self.resultId = defined
		self.lhs = left
		self.imm = immediate
		self.pred = cmpPred
		self.operandType = readType
	end 'recordCmpImm'

	export function isBinaryArith() returns bool
		return self.shape == StdArithShape.binaryArith
	end 'isBinaryArith'

	export function isComparison() returns bool
		return self.shape == StdArithShape.comparison
	end 'isComparison'

	export function isUnary() returns bool
		return self.shape == StdArithShape.unary
	end 'isUnary'

	// The IMMEDIATE binary-arithmetic shape — `binOpImm`, an `opcode` over `lhs` and `imm`. Named
	// beside `isBinaryArith` because a reader that cares WHICH binary shape it has cannot ask
	// `hasImmediate()`: that is true of `comparisonImm` too, whose `opcode` field was never written
	// (see the header — the fields are meaningless until the shape says which were filled).
	export function isBinaryArithImm() returns bool
		return self.shape == StdArithShape.binaryArithImm
	end 'isBinaryArithImm'

	// Whether this record describes an arithmetic op at all — the admission test for a pass whose
	// subject is EVERY shape rather than one of them.
	export function isArithmetic() returns bool
		return self.shape != StdArithShape.neither
	end 'isArithmetic'

	// Whether the shape reads TWO value operands. `unary` and the two immediate forms read one; an
	// immediate is an encoded literal and not a value at all.
	export function hasSecondValueOperand() returns bool
		return self.shape == StdArithShape.binaryArith or self.shape == StdArithShape.comparison
	end 'hasSecondValueOperand'

	// Whether the shape carries an ENCODED IMMEDIATE where the other binary shapes carry a second value
	// operand. The complement `hasSecondValueOperand` leaves for a shape that IS arithmetic, so between
	// the two a reader can say what the second half of the expression is without reading `shape`.
	export function hasImmediate() returns bool
		return self.shape == StdArithShape.binaryArithImm or self.shape == StdArithShape.comparisonImm
	end 'hasImmediate'

	// Rewrite this record's VALUE OPERANDS through a substitution column, leaving `resultId` alone (a
	// def is not a use — the same split `remapStdOpUses` makes, for the same reason).
	//
	// It is what lets a walk compare an op against one whose reads it has already decided to rewrite:
	// the column is the pass's PENDING rewrite, and an operand read through it is the value the op will
	// read once that rewrite is applied.
	export function remapOperandsThrough(subst ValueIdArray)
		if not self.isArithmetic() 'nothingToRemap'
			return
		end 'nothingToRemap'

		self.lhs = remappedValue(self.lhs, subst: subst)

		if self.hasSecondValueOperand() 'twoValueOperands'
			self.rhs = remappedValue(self.rhs, subst: subst)
		end 'twoValueOperands'
	end 'remapOperandsThrough'

	// ⭐ **PUT A COMMUTATIVE OP'S TWO OPERANDS IN A CANONICAL ORDER, SO `a + b` AND `b + a` COMPARE
	// EQUAL.** The lower `ValueId` lands on the lhs — an arbitrary but TOTAL order over a function's
	// dense value space, which is all a canonical form needs.
	//
	// ⚠ **FLOATS ARE EXCLUDED, AND THE REASON IS NOT `binOpcodeIsCommutative`'s.** That function answers
	// `true` for float `add`/`mul` and is right to: `a + b` and `b + a` are the same REAL number. What
	// they are not is the same BITS when an operand is a NaN — x64's `addsd`/`mulsd` answer with the
	// destination operand's NaN, so a swap can change which payload propagates. No Maxon program can
	// print a payload, so this is a difference nothing observes; it is excluded anyway, because a
	// canonical form that is only unobservably wrong is a trap for the next pass that reads it.
	//
	// ⚠ It answers for `binaryArith` ALONE. A `comparison` has its own swap rule (`a < b` is `b > a`,
	// which needs the predicate flipped too — `foldConstOperands.flipCmpPred`), and neither immediate
	// form has a second operand to swap.
	export function canonicalizeCommutativeOperands()
		if not self.isBinaryArith() 'notATwoOperandArith'
			return
		end 'notATwoOperandArith'

		if stdTypeIsFloat(self.operandType) or not binOpcodeIsCommutative(self.opcode) 'mustKeepItsOrder'
			return
		end 'mustKeepItsOrder'

		if self.lhs <= self.rhs 'alreadyCanonical'
			return
		end 'alreadyCanonical'

		let lower = self.rhs
		self.rhs = self.lhs
		self.lhs = lower
	end 'canonicalizeCommutativeOperands'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE EXPRESSION-EQUALITY PREDICATE, AND IT LIVES ON THE RECORD THAT DEFINES WHAT AN
	// EXPRESSION IS.** True when two records describe computations producing the same value from the
	// same inputs — every field the shape says is meaningful must agree, and `resultId` must not, since
	// two occurrences of one expression are exactly two names for one value.
	//
	// `operandType` is compared even though every integer form computes at the MACHINE WORD on all
	// three targets: the type is what a wasm local's class is declared from (`wasmValTypeOf` and
	// `wasmMachineValTypeOf` disagree for the narrow integers), so two ops differing only in it are
	// deliberately left as two.
	//
	// A `neither` record equals nothing, itself included: it describes no computation, so there is no
	// value for a second one to be the same as.
	export function computesSameValueAs(other StdArithOperands) returns bool
		if not self.isArithmetic() or self.shape != other.shape 'notOneExpression'
			return false
		end 'notOneExpression'

		if self.lhs != other.lhs or self.operandType != other.operandType 'differentInputs'
			return false
		end 'differentInputs'

		return match self.shape 'shape'
			// Unreachable — the guard above already returned — but answered rather than defaulted, so
			// the arm stays correct if that guard is ever moved.
			neither gives false
			binaryArith gives self.rhs == other.rhs and self.opcode == other.opcode
			comparison gives self.rhs == other.rhs and self.pred == other.pred
			unary gives self.unaryOpcode == other.unaryOpcode
			binaryArithImm gives self.imm == other.imm and self.opcode == other.opcode
			comparisonImm gives self.imm == other.imm and self.pred == other.pred
		end 'shape'
	end 'computesSameValueAs'
end 'StdArithOperands'

// ⭐⭐ **WHETHER SWAPPING A `binOp`'s TWO OPERANDS CHANGES ITS ANSWER — THE one place that is stated.**
// It lives beside `StdBinOpcode` rather than inside a pass for `stdBinOpcodeIsFloatOnly`'s reason: it
// is a property OF THE OPCODE and of no one target or pass. Three readers today —
// `foldConstOperands.tryFoldBinOp` (may it move a const lhs onto the rhs?),
// `StdArithOperands.canonicalizeCommutativeOperands` (may CSE order the two operands?) and
// `StdToX64Conversion.commutesForCoalescing` (may the ISEL put the dying operand on the left?) — and
// they must not answer it separately.
//
// ⚠ `export`, NOT `module`, SINCE EC19: the third reader is in `Compiler/Targets/X64/`, and `module`
// visibility is a DIRECTORY SUBTREE. The alternative was a second commutativity roster at the target
// tier, which is the one thing this function exists to prevent — and it would have had to re-derive
// the `min`/`max` answer below, which is exactly the one a second copy gets wrong.
export function binOpcodeIsCommutative(opcode StdBinOpcode) returns bool
	return match opcode 'o'
		add gives true
		mul gives true
		sub gives false
		bitAnd to bitXor gives true
		shl to shrLogical gives false
		// EC18 — `mulhi(a, b)` is `mulhi(b, a)`: the 128-bit product is commutative and taking its top
		// half does not change that. ⚠ Answering it here costs `commutesForCoalescing` nothing, because
		// that reader asks `integerBinOpIsTwoAddress` as well and x64's `imul r/m64` is a FIXED-REGISTER
		// form rather than a two-address one — so no swap is ever taken on this opcode today. The answer
		// is still the opcode's own, and stating it any other way would make this roster lie.
		mulHighSigned gives true
		// The three FLOAT-ONLY opcodes:
		//   • `div` — `a / b` is not `b / a`.
		//   • `min`/`max` — `min(a, b)` is `a` when `a < b` and `b` otherwise, `max(a, b)` is `a` when
		//     `a > b` and `b` otherwise, and a STRICT comparison is FALSE on unordered — so `min(nan, 5.0)`
		//     is `5.0` while `min(5.0, nan)` is `nan`, and `max` answers the same pair. A swap changes the
		//     answer. v1 excludes its own `minF64`/`maxF64` from commuting for exactly this reason
		//     (`CommuteForCoalescing.maxon:269`). See `StdBinOpcode`'s `min`/`max` header.
		div to max gives false
	end 'o'
end 'binOpcodeIsCommutative'

// Fill `operands` with the arithmetic shape `op` carries. Exhaustive over StdOp, no bare `default`.
//
// ⚠ The last arm is pinned to the union's CURRENT LAST variant, so a variant APPENDED at the end
// (the rule `StdOp`'s header states) falls outside every arm and is a COMPILE ERROR here rather
// than a silently-missed classification.
export function classifyArithOperands(op StdOp, operands StdArithOperands)
	match op 'b'
		const then operands.recordNeither()
		binOp(resultId, lhs, rhs, opcode, operandType) then operands.recordBinOp(resultId, left: lhs, right: rhs, binOpcode: opcode, readType: operandType)
		unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, operandType) then operands.recordUnaryOp(resultId, only: operand, unaryOpcodeRead: opcode, readType: operandType)
		cmp(resultId, lhs, rhs, pred, operandType) then operands.recordCmp(resultId, left: lhs, right: rhs, cmpPred: pred, readType: operandType)
		// The IMMEDIATE forms — what `foldConstOperands` PRODUCES, which is why that pass cannot want
		// them as INPUT (its question, "is an operand a const?", has no second operand to ask it of).
		// They are still real arithmetic, and CSE reads them as such.
		binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode, operandType) then operands.recordBinOpImm(resultId, left: lhs, immediate: imm, binOpcode: opcode, readType: operandType)
		cmpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, pred, operandType) then operands.recordCmpImm(resultId, left: lhs, immediate: imm, cmpPred: pred, readType: operandType)
		// Everything from the two DIVISIONS to the end of the union. Two bands are worth naming, because
		// each is excluded for its OWN reason rather than by falling off the end of the list:
		//   • `div`/`mod` — `isPure: false`. x64 `idiv` faults on a zero divisor and on `i64.min / -1`,
		//     so they may be neither folded away, nor dropped when unread, nor answered once for two
		//     occurrences; they are not a rewrite subject at any tier of this compiler.
		//   • everything else — terminators, calls, memory and the OS band define call returns, kernel
		//     answers and addresses, none of which is an arithmetic shape at all.
		div to osEnterBackgroundPriority then operands.recordNeither()
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then operands.recordNeither()
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then operands.recordNeither()
		// The ordered memory band (G3) — memory accesses, so no arithmetic shape, exactly as their plain
		// twins have none.
		loadAcquire or storeRelease then operands.recordNeither()
		osHandleFileType then operands.recordNeither()
	end 'b'
end 'classifyArithOperands'

// ⛔⛔ **THE CHECK BETWEEN THE ARITHMETIC ROSTER ABOVE AND THE PURITY FLAG, because the two statements
// would otherwise be free to disagree.** `classifyArithOperands` decides which variants are ARITHMETIC;
// `StdOpMeta.isPure` decides which may be dropped, duplicated or reordered. Every variant the roster
// admits declares `isPure: true` today, and nothing enforces it: a future op appended to the arith band
// with `isPure: false` (another trapping form, say) would be given an arm in the roster by a reader
// thinking about SHAPE, and a code-motion pass would move it without ever asking. Asking here costs one
// field read per arithmetic op and turns that into a panic on the first program that compiles it.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT THE MEMORY GATE.** A load never reaches here — it is `StdArithShape.neither` — so
// flipping `loadIndirect` to `isPure: true` (the sabotage `StdOpMeta.isPure`'s own comment records)
// still would not make a pass treat one as arithmetic. The roster refuses it first.
//
// `reader` names the pass, because the panic is about a disagreement between the dialect and whoever
// asked, and a message that could not say which pass tripped it would send the reader to this file
// rather than to the roster arm that admitted the op.
module function requireArithOpIsPure(op StdOp, reader String)
	if not op.rawValue.isPure 'impure'
		panic("{reader}: `classifyArithOperands` admitted an op in category {op.rawValue.category.name} that the dialect declares `isPure: false` — an op that may trap or have effects must never be dropped, duplicated or moved")
	end 'impure'
end 'requireArithOpIsPure'

// The operands of a `loadIndirect`, written into a caller-owned record — what `StdArithOperands` is for
// the arithmetic band, for the ONE memory op a pass may want to move.
//
// ⭐ **IT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN THE PASS, for the reason the arithmetic roster does.** A `match` over
// `StdOp` inside a pass is a roster that can drift from the union; here the range arms are pinned to the
// union's current FIRST and LAST variants, so a variant APPENDED at the end (the rule `StdOp`'s header
// states) keeps being classified as "not a load" and a variant INSERTED mid-band fails to compile.
//
// ⚠ **THE FIELDS ARE MEANINGLESS UNTIL `isLoad` SAYS THE RECORD WAS FILLED**, and none may be read as a
// sentinel — ValueId 0, offset 0 and `StdType.i64` are all real. Same discipline as `StdArithOperands`.
module type StdLoadOperands
	var load as bool
	export var resultId as ValueId
	// The ADDRESS the load reads through. `offset` is a field displacement from it, never a separate
	// value — which is what lets a reader compare two loads through one address by offset alone.
	export var addrId as ValueId
	export var offset as ByteOffset
	export var valueType as StdType

	module static function create() returns StdLoadOperands
		return Self{load: false, resultId: 0, addrId: 0, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64}
	end 'create'

	export function isLoad() returns bool
		return self.load
	end 'isLoad'

	// How far past `addrId` this load reads — `offset` plus the width it transfers. The number a
	// dereferenceability argument is made in: a load the program performs proves the object at `addrId`
	// spans at least this many bytes.
	export function coverage() returns LoadCoverage
		if not self.load 'notALoad'
			panic("StdLoadOperands.coverage: this record does not describe a load — ask `isLoad` first")
		end 'notALoad'

		return self.offset + stdTypeStorageBytes(self.valueType)
	end 'coverage'

	function recordNotALoad()
		self.load = false
	end 'recordNotALoad'

	function recordLoad(defined ValueId, address ValueId, displacement ByteOffset, readType StdType)
		self.load = true
		self.resultId = defined
		self.addrId = address
		self.offset = displacement
		self.valueType = readType
	end 'recordLoad'
end 'StdLoadOperands'

// How many bytes past its address a load reads — an ordinary non-negative byte count, named because
// `ByteOffset` is where the read STARTS and this is where it ENDS.
module typealias LoadCoverage = int(0 to u64.max)

// Fill `load` with the memory operands `op` carries, or with "not a load". Exhaustive over StdOp, no
// bare `default` — see `StdLoadOperands` for why the two range arms are pinned where they are.
module function classifyLoadOperands(op StdOp, load StdLoadOperands)
	match op 'l'
		const to globalAddr then load.recordNotALoad()
		loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) then load.recordLoad(resultId, address: addrId, displacement: offset, readType: valueType)
		storeIndirect to osEnterBackgroundPriority then load.recordNotALoad()
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then load.recordNotALoad()
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then load.recordNotALoad()
		// ⛔⛔ **AN ACQUIRE LOAD IS `recordNotALoad`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF IT.** This classifier's
		// one reader is `loopInvariantCodeMotion`, which HOISTS a load whose address the loop does not
		// write. A green thread's status word is written by another OS THREAD, never by the loop that spins
		// on it, so the hoist test would answer "invariant" and the drive loop would read one stale word for
		// ever. That is precisely the reordering `loadAcquire` exists to forbid, so it must not be offered
		// to the pass as a hoistable load at all. `storeRelease` is not a load in the first place.
		loadAcquire or storeRelease then load.recordNotALoad()
		osHandleFileType then load.recordNotALoad()
	end 'l'
end 'classifyLoadOperands'

// The set of value ids a function's ops READ. Every Std-tier pass that asks "does anything
// still read this value?" — the const DCE, the dead-block-arg prune, the trivial-block-arg
// elimination — reads it from `collectStdOpUses` below rather than re-matching the union, so a
// new StdOp variant is a compile error in ONE place instead of a silently-missed use at each of
// them. (This is the Std tier's counterpart to `targetOpOperands`, which plays the same role for
// the Target tier's liveness, splitter, colorer and checker.)
//
// A DENSE COLUMN, not a `Set`. A ValueId IS a dense, function-local index — so a hash set spends a
// hash and a probe to look up something that is already an array subscript. That is the design's
// "NO `Set`/`Map` and NO hashing in the hot path" rule (ARCHITECTURE.md §"Data representation")
// broken in the one place it is easiest to break it, and on the corpus's single large function it
// showed: `foldConstOperands` grew at exponent **1.29 in TIME while its ALLOCATIONS grew at 0.99** —
// the signature of a structure whose asymptotics are fine and whose CONSTANT degrades, because every
// probe into a table that doubles with the program is a cache miss that the previous rung did not
// take. It was invisible while the register allocator's quadratic dwarfed it at 87% of the compile;
// removing that made this the phase over its budget.
//
// It needs no size up front — the column grows to fit the highest id it is given — so it is a drop-in
// for the `Set` it replaces, with the same three operations and no hashing at all.
export type StdValueUseSet
	var member as BoolArray
	// ⭐ THE SECOND READING THE SAME WALK CAN GIVE: which ids were inserted MORE THAN ONCE — "this
	// value has another reader", which is the admission test of every fold that CONSUMES a value
	// rather than merely reading it (EC16's scaled-index address is the first).
	//
	// ⚠ THE **GROWTH** IS OPT-IN, NOT THE FIELD — and the difference is measured. A second `BoolArray`
	// over the function's whole value space is real memory, and nine of this type's ten users only ever
	// ask `contains`, so `create` leaves `tracksRepeats` false and `insert` never GROWS the column for
	// them. What they still pay is the empty `BoolArray.create()` in the constructor: `scale-test`
	// charges that ~25,000 allocations at rung 5, one per `StdValueUseSet.create()`, spread over
	// `foldConstOperands` / `foldConstants` / `promoteStackRecords` / `pruneDeadBlockArgs` / `isel`
	// (see `docs/optimization-log.md`'s EC16 row). It is +0.06% of a compile against the −1.59% the
	// fold this column serves buys back — stated because an unexplained number in that table is the
	// thing the instrument exists to prevent, not because it is large.
	var tracksRepeats as bool
	var repeated as BoolArray

	export static function create() returns StdValueUseSet
		return Self{member: BoolArray.create(), tracksRepeats: false, repeated: BoolArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// The counting form — same set, plus `containsRepeat`. See `tracksRepeats`.
	export static function createTrackingRepeats() returns StdValueUseSet
		return Self{member: BoolArray.create(), tracksRepeats: true, repeated: BoolArray.create()}
	end 'createTrackingRepeats'

	// Empty the set, KEEPING the column's capacity — for a per-function set that is hoisted out of
	// the function loop rather than re-created inside it (`Array.clear` keeps capacity, so the second
	// function's inserts reuse the first's buffer and the whole walk allocates once per compilation).
	export function clear()
		self.member.clear()
		self.repeated.clear()
	end 'clear'

	// Whether `valueId` was inserted MORE THAN ONCE — i.e. some second op, branch edge or second
	// operand of the same op also reads it.
	//
	// It PANICS on a set that was not created counting, rather than answering `false`: a reader that
	// asks this of a plain `create()` set is asking a question the walk never recorded, and `false`
	// would mean "nothing else reads it" — the permissive answer, which for a consuming fold is a
	// WRONG ANSWER and not a missed one.
	export function containsRepeat(valueId ValueId) returns bool
		if not self.tracksRepeats 'notCounting'
			panic("StdValueUseSet.containsRepeat: this set was built by `create()`, which records membership only — a reader that needs multiplicity must build it with `createTrackingRepeats()`")
		end 'notCounting'
		if valueId >= self.repeated.count() 'neverRepeated'
			return false
		end 'neverRepeated'
		return try self.repeated.get(valueId) otherwise panic("StdValueUseSet.containsRepeat: repeated.get OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'containsRepeat'

	export function insert(valueId ValueId)
		// `growFilled`, NOT `resize`: this column is extended ONE ENTRY AT A TIME (inserting ids in
		// increasing order is exactly what the collect walk does), and `resize` reserves EXACTLY the length
		// it is given — Θ(values²) of allocator traffic for a column that ends up Θ(values) long. Measured,
		// on the first cut of this very type: +4 GB at scale rung 5, with the byte curve at exponent 2.0.
		// `growFilled` grows the capacity geometrically and clears what the growth added.
		self.member.growFilled(valueId + 1, value: false)

		if self.tracksRepeats and self.contains(valueId) 'secondReader'
			self.repeated.growFilled(valueId + 1, value: false)
			try self.repeated.set(valueId, value: true) otherwise panic("StdValueUseSet.insert: repeated.set OOB at {valueId}")
		end 'secondReader'

		try self.member.set(valueId, value: true) otherwise panic("StdValueUseSet.insert: member.set OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'insert'

	export function contains(valueId ValueId) returns bool
		if valueId >= self.member.count() 'neverInserted'
			return false
		end 'neverInserted'
		return try self.member.get(valueId) otherwise panic("StdValueUseSet.contains: member.get OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'contains'

	// Whether NOTHING has been inserted. The column is only ever extended by `insert`, so an empty
	// backing store is an exact answer rather than an approximation of one — which is what lets
	// `removeOpsDefining` skip a function's whole block walk when there is nothing to remove.
	export function isEmpty() returns bool
		return self.member.count() == 0
	end 'isEmpty'
end 'StdValueUseSet'

// ⭐ EVERY VALUE ID A FUNCTION READS — every operand of every live op, its terminator included, plus
// every value passed on a phi branch edge. The whole-function wrapper around `collectStdOpUses`, whose
// contract stops at ONE op and deliberately excludes the branch-edge args (they live on the
// predecessor's `IrBlock`, not on any op), so a caller that forgot the edges would silently believe a
// phi operand had no reader.
//
// The set is HANDED IN rather than returned, which is what lets a caller choose the counting form
// (`StdValueUseSet.createTrackingRepeats`) or reuse one buffer across every function of a module.
//
// It walks `func.blockRefs` directly rather than `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, which visits exactly the
// same ops and allocates a fresh `OpIndexArray` to say so.
export function collectFunctionValueUses(module StdModule, func IrFunction, referenced StdValueUseSet)
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("collectFunctionValueUses: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			collectStdOpUses(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectFunctionValueUses: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), referenced: referenced)
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
			collectStdOpUses(try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("collectFunctionValueUses: terminatorIndex not in module.ops"), referenced: referenced)
		end 'hasTerm'

		for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
			for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
				referenced.insert(argId)
			end 'eachArg'
		end 'eachEdge'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'collectFunctionValueUses'

// Add the value ids `op` READS to `referenced` — its DEFS are not uses, and neither are the
// branch-edge args (those live on the predecessor's `IrBlock`, not on the op). Exhaustive
// over StdOp, no bare `default`.
//
// EXPORTED, not module-scoped: the Std→x64 lowering reads it too, to decide which compare
// results are read as VALUES (and so need a `setcc`) rather than only as branch FLAGS. That
// is exactly the question this function exists to answer in one place — a second, private
// "what does this op read?" match inside the backend is the drift it was written to prevent.
export function collectStdOpUses(op StdOp, referenced StdValueUseSet)
	match op 'u'
		const then break 'u'
		binOp(_, lhs, rhs, _, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		unaryOp(_, operand, _, _) then referenced.insert(operand)
		cmp(_, lhs, rhs, _, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		binOpImm(_, lhs, _, _, _) then referenced.insert(lhs)
		cmpImm(_, lhs, _, _, _) then referenced.insert(lhs)
		div(_, lhs, rhs, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		mod(_, lhs, rhs, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		condBranch(condId, _, _) then referenced.insert(condId)
		branch then break 'u'
		ret(retValId) then referenced.insert(retValId)
		param then break 'u'
		call(_, _, args) then insertUseArgs(referenced, args: args)
		retVoid then break 'u'
		// `globalAddr` reads nothing — its `label` is a NAME, not a value, so it has no
		// operand at all (its result is a def). The other two read the address they are
		// handed, and a store reads the value it writes as well.
		globalAddr then break 'u'
		loadIndirect(_, addrId, _, _) then referenced.insert(addrId)
		storeIndirect(addrId, _, valueId, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: addrId, b: valueId)
		// The size is a real value operand — the only one this op reads; its result is a def.
		osAllocPages(_, sizeId) then referenced.insert(sizeId)
		// Both the buffer and the length are real value operands; the byte count is a def.
		osWriteStdout(_, bufId, lenId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufId, b: lenId)
		// `rdataAddr` reads nothing — its `label` is a NAME, not a value (its result is a def), exactly
		// like `globalAddr`.
		rdataAddr then break 'u'
		// A throwing call reads its arguments exactly as `call` does; `resultId`/`errorFlagId` are DEFS.
		tryCall(_, _, _, args) then insertUseArgs(referenced, args: args)
		// The dual-register terminator reads BOTH the primary value and the error flag it writes out.
		errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: retValId, b: errorFlagId)
		// The exit code is the one value operand `osExit` reads.
		osExit(codeId) then referenced.insert(codeId)
		// `funcAddr` reads nothing — its `funcName` is a NAME, not a value (its result is a def), exactly
		// like `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr`.
		funcAddr then break 'u'
		// An indirect call reads the callee register AND its arguments; `resultId` is a def, and
		// `resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata (not value operands).
		callIndirect(_, calleeReg, _, _, args) then insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced, calleeReg: calleeReg, args: args)
		// A range panic reads nothing — its `label` is a NAME (an `.rdata` symbol), not a value, and it
		// defines nothing.
		osPanic then break 'u'
		// Both out-param ADDRESSES are real value operands (the builder's `globalAddr` results); this op
		// defines nothing. The `ms` count is the one value `osSleepMs` reads. Both must be reported here or
		// the allocator would free the address/count registers early and the OS call would read garbage.
		osReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: ticksPtrId, b: freqPtrId)
		osSleepMs(msId) then referenced.insert(msId)
		// P1.5 subprocess: each reads its operands and (except `osWaitHandle`) defines nothing. `osWaitHandle`'s
		// `resultId` is a DEF, so it is skipped here exactly as `osAllocPages`'s result id is.
		osProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtr, cwdPtr, creationFlags, startupPtr, procInfoPtr, envPtr) then insertUseSextet(referenced, a: cmdlinePtr, b: cwdPtr, c: creationFlags, d: startupPtr, e: procInfoPtr, f: envPtr)
		osProcessExitCode(handle, codePtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: codePtr)
		osWaitHandles(handleArrayPtr, count, timeoutMs) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: handleArrayPtr, b: count, c: timeoutMs)
		osCloseHandle(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		osWaitHandle(_, handle, timeoutMs) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: timeoutMs)
		// The region base AND its length are both value operands; it defines nothing (like `osExit`/`osCloseHandle`).
		// The size is reported even though x64-windows reads it nowhere — this set is target-neutral, and a POSIX
		// `munmap` would find its length register freed early if it were not.
		osFreePages(addr, size) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: addr, b: size)
		// The assembled message's address is the one value operand a rendered panic reads; it defines
		// nothing (the label-carrying `osPanic` reads nothing at all).
		osPanicValue(messagePtr) then referenced.insert(messagePtr)
		// No operands: `GetCommandLineA` reads the PEB.
		osCommandLine then break 'u'
		// The jump table reads ONLY the biased index `subjectId`; `denseTargetBlockIds`/`defaultBlockId` are
		// block ids (the CFG, carried on the op), NOT value operands, and are deliberately not bound here (a
		// tail-slot Array binding in this big match is the decref-corruption trap `switch` documents).
		switch(subjectId, _, _) then referenced.insert(subjectId)
		// A witness call reads the WITNESS-TABLE ADDRESS and its arguments — the exact shape of `callIndirect`
		// (`witnessAddr` in place of the callee register), so it reports uses the same way. `resultId` is a def;
		// `methodIndex`/`resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata (not value operands).
		witnessCall(_, witnessAddr, _, _, _, args) then insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced, calleeReg: witnessAddr, args: args)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: each IOCP op reports its VALUE operands; its `resultId` (where present) is a DEF,
		// skipped here exactly as `osWaitHandle`'s is. `osIocpCreate`/`osEventCreate`/`osLastError` read nothing (a
		// pure result def, like `osAllocPages`'s but with no size operand).
		osPipeCreate(namePtr, saPtr, iocp, readPtr, writePtr) then insertUseQuint(referenced, a: namePtr, b: saPtr, c: iocp, d: readPtr, e: writePtr)
		osIocpCreate then break 'u'
		osEventCreate then break 'u'
		osThreadCreate(_, entryPtr, arg) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: entryPtr, b: arg)
		osLockInit(lockPtr) then referenced.insert(lockPtr)
		osLockEnter(lockPtr) then referenced.insert(lockPtr)
		osLockLeave(lockPtr) then referenced.insert(lockPtr)
		osReadOverlapped(_, handle, buf, cap, bytesOutPtr, ovl) then insertUseQuint(referenced, a: handle, b: buf, c: cap, d: bytesOutPtr, e: ovl)
		osLastError then break 'u'
		osProcessSpawnInherit(cmdlinePtr, cwdPtr, creationFlags, startupPtr, procInfoPtr, envPtr) then insertUseSextet(referenced, a: cmdlinePtr, b: cwdPtr, c: creationFlags, d: startupPtr, e: procInfoPtr, f: envPtr)
		osCancelIo(handle, ovl) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: ovl)
		osGetPid then break 'u'
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: the streaming stdin pipe reads four value operands (its two out-pointers are
		// still live operands); `osWriteFile` reads handle+buf+len+bytesOutPtr, its `resultId` a def skipped here.
		osPipeCreateOutbound(namePtr, saPtr, childReadPtr, parentWritePtr) then insertUseQuad(referenced, a: namePtr, b: saPtr, c: childReadPtr, d: parentWritePtr)
		osWriteFile(_, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) then insertUseQuad(referenced, a: handle, b: buf, c: len, d: bytesOutPtr)
		// The out-param ADDRESS is the one value operand the wall-clock read has, and it must be reported or
		// the allocator would free its register before the OS call writes through it — the same obligation
		// `osReadClock`'s two addresses carry. It defines nothing.
		osReadWallClock(ftPtr) then referenced.insert(ftPtr)
		// P1.7a slice 2b-v: the THROWING witness call reads exactly what `witnessCall` reads — the witness-table
		// address and its arguments — through the SAME helper. Both its results (`resultId`, `errorFlagId`) are
		// defs, exactly as `tryCall`'s two are.
		witnessTryCall(_, _, witnessAddr, _, _, _, args) then insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced, calleeReg: witnessAddr, args: args)
		// R4.1: the file OPEN reads its path BUFFER address; `resultId` is a def and is skipped here.
		osFileOpenRead(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		// The close reads its HANDLE and defines nothing.
		osFileClose(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		// R4.2. Each reads its VALUE operands (an out-param ADDRESS is a live operand for the reason
		// `osReadWallClock`'s is: the allocator must not free its register before the OS writes through it);
		// every `resultId` is a def and is skipped here.
		osReadFile(_, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) then insertUseQuad(referenced, a: handle, b: buf, c: len, d: bytesOutPtr)
		osFileOpenWrite(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		osEnvBlock then break 'u'
		osEnvBlockFree(blockPtr) then referenced.insert(blockPtr)
		osHandleFileType(_, handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		osFileSize(_, handle, sizeOutPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: sizeOutPtr)
		osFileDelete(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		osFileRename(_, oldPathPtr, newPathPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: oldPathPtr, b: newPathPtr)
		osFileFindFirst(_, pathPtr, findDataPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: pathPtr, b: findDataPtr)
		osFileFindClose(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		osFileFindNext(_, handle, findDataPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: findDataPtr)
		osDirCreate(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		osDirCurrentPath(_, bufLen, bufPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufLen, b: bufPtr)
		osFileAttributes(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		// W5, widened at the subprocess rung. ⚠ **THIS ARM READ "No operands: the `STD_INPUT_HANDLE` selector
		// is a backend immediate, so this reads nothing at all" — and the line beneath it already reported one.**
		// The SELECTOR is a value operand now (`OutputDestination.inherit` asks for the other two streams), and
		// reporting it is not optional: unreported, the allocator would free its register before `GetStdHandle`
		// read it. Its `resultId` is a def and is skipped here.
		osStdHandle(_, which) then referenced.insert(which)
		// Both the buffer ADDRESS and the LENGTH are live value operands, for the reason `osDirCurrentPath`'s
		// pair are: the allocator must not free either register before the OS writes through the first.
		osExecutablePath(_, bufLen, bufPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufLen, b: bufPtr)
		// W35: `osWriteStdout`'s arm one stream over, and it reads exactly what that one reads.
		osWriteStderr(_, bufId, lenId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufId, b: lenId)
		// A promoted record's address reads NOTHING — its `slot` is a frame index, not a value, exactly as
		// `globalAddr`'s `label` is a name. Its `resultId` is a def.
		stackRecordAddr then break 'u'
		// O3: the segment open reads three pointers/counts, the map reads a handle and a size, and the tick
		// read has no operand at all.
		osOpenNamedSegment(_, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: namePtr, b: bufPtr, c: bufLen)
		osMapNamedSegment(_, handle, size) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: size)
		osTickCountMs then break 'u'
		// The four attached-subprocess host primitives, each reporting exactly its VALUE operands; the two that
		// answer in RAX (`osPipePeek`, `osEnvRead`) have `resultId` as a DEF, skipped here exactly as
		// `osWaitHandle`'s is.
		osHandleSetInherit(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		osPipePeek(_, handle, availPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: availPtr)
		osEnvRead(_, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: namePtr, b: bufPtr, c: bufLen)
		osProcessTerminate(handle, exitCode) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: exitCode)
		// The eight socket host primitives, each reporting exactly its VALUE operands; the six that answer in
		// RAX have `resultId` as a DEF, skipped here exactly as `osPipePeek`'s is.
		// ⚠ `osSocketCreate` reads NOTHING — its three arguments are fixed Win32 constants the lowering
		// supplies, so it takes a `break` arm like `osTickCountMs` rather than naming an operand it has not got.
		osSocketStartup(_, wsaDataPtr) then referenced.insert(wsaDataPtr)
		osSocketResolve(_, hostPtr, hintsPtr, outPtr) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: hostPtr, b: hintsPtr, c: outPtr)
		osSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtr) then referenced.insert(addrInfoPtr)
		osSocketCreate then break 'u'
		osSocketConnect(_, sock, addrPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: sock, b: addrPtr)
		osSocketSend(_, sock, bufPtr, len) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: sock, b: bufPtr, c: len)
		osSocketRecv(_, sock, bufPtr, len) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: sock, b: bufPtr, c: len)
		osSocketClose(sock) then referenced.insert(sock)
		// `osCpuCount` takes NO operands — it asks the host a question rather than reading a value, so it
		// joins `osSocketCreate` and `osGetPid` as one of the few system ops with nothing to collect.
		osCpuCount then break 'u'
		// The out-param ADDRESS is the one value operand the thread-CPU read has, and it must be reported for
		// `osReadWallClock`'s reason: the allocator would otherwise free its register before the OS call
		// writes through it. It defines nothing.
		osThreadCpuTicks(outPtr) then referenced.insert(outPtr)
		// F1a: base and length are BOTH real value operands of the decommit — the same pair `osFreePages`
		// reports, and reported for the same reason: the allocator would otherwise free the length's register
		// before the OS call reads it.
		osDecommitPages(addr, size) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: addr, b: size)
		// F1b: the destination and the length are the two value operands a fill reads; the fill BYTE is a
		// compile-time constant on the op, so it is not one. It defines nothing.
		memFill(dest, byteCount, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: dest, b: byteCount)
		// F1c: a commit reads base and length, for the decommit's reason; a reservation reads only the length,
		// exactly as `osAllocPages` does. `resultId` is a DEF on both, so it is skipped here exactly as
		// `osAllocPages`'s result id is.
		osCommitPages(_, addr, size) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: addr, b: size)
		osReservePages(_, sizeId) then referenced.insert(sizeId)
		// F2: every operand of an atomic is a genuine value READ — the address, plus the words compared and
		// stored — so all of them must be reported, or the allocator frees a register the instruction still
		// needs. Each `resultId` is a DEF, reported by `stdOpDefinedValue` instead.
		atomicCas(_, addr, expected, replacement) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: addr, b: expected, c: replacement)
		atomicRmw(_, addr, operand, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: addr, b: operand)
		// The thread token reads no VALUE at all — its input is the OS thread it is asked on. It defines one.
		currentThreadToken then break 'u'
		// G1: `tlsAlloc` asks the OS for a slot and reads no value; the other three each read every operand
		// they are given. `tlsSlotLoad`'s `tebOffset` is a genuine register read — it is the ADDRESS the `gs:`
		// load indexes by — so omitting it would free a register the instruction still needs.
		tlsAlloc then break 'u'
		tlsSetValue(index, value) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: index, b: value)
		tlsSlotLoad(_, tebOffset) then referenced.insert(tebOffset)
		osSetEvent(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		// The priority door takes NO operands — it asks the OS to change this process and then asks what it
		// became, exactly as `osCpuCount` asks a question rather than reading a value.
		osEnterBackgroundPriority then break 'u'
		// G3: the ordered band reads exactly what its plain twin reads — the address, plus the value a
		// store writes. Both `resultId`s are DEFS, reported by `stdOpDefinedValue`.
		loadAcquire(_, addrId, _, _) then referenced.insert(addrId)
		storeRelease(addrId, _, valueId, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: addrId, b: valueId)
	end 'u'
end 'collectStdOpUses'

// ⭐ **WHAT AN OP DEFINES — the exact dual of `collectStdOpUses` above, and it lives beside it for that
// reason.** `nextFreeValueId` is the SHARED one: it began as `InsertRangeChecks`' private helper and
// `promoteStackRecords` needs the same answer, and a second "one past the highest id in this function"
// would be a second chance to hand out an id something already defines. `stdOpDefinedValue` below is
// still read only by `nextFreeValueId` — it is not exported, and it is here because the answer it gives
// belongs beside `collectStdOpUses`' rather than inside one pass that happens to have needed it first.
// The next unused ValueId in the function: one past the highest id any op DEFINES or any block-arg
// carries. Scanning defs (and phi results) suffices — dense SSA numbers every value once, so the
// maximum def id is the maximum id, and a use can only name an id that some def already introduced.
export function nextFreeValueId(module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns ValueId
	var maxId = 0 as ValueId
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("nextFreeValueId: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachArg'
			maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: arg.valueId)
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachBlock'
	for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("nextFreeValueId: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		match stdOpDefinedValue(op) 'defines'
			none then break 'defines'
			defines(definedId) then maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: definedId)
			definesPair(first, second) then maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: maxValueId(first, other: second))
		end 'defines'
	end 'eachOp'
	return maxId + 1
end 'nextFreeValueId'

function maxValueId(a ValueId, other ValueId) returns ValueId
	return a if a >= other else other
end 'maxValueId'

// Whether `op` DEFINES a value, and WHICH — ONE exhaustive `StdOp` match answering both halves.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT WAS TWO, AND WHAT KEPT THEM AGREEING WAS NOTHING** (P1.9 review). `stdOpDefinesValue`
// answered "does it define?" over a range-arm partition of the union and `stdOpDefinedIdOrZero`
// answered "which id?" over a destructured one — ~105 lines of parallel arms a reader had to diff by
// eye. A new `StdOp` variant is a compile error in BOTH, which is what the exhaustive match buys; but
// adding it as `true` in the first and `0` in the second COMPILES, and the consequence is not a
// diagnostic: `nextFreeValueId` under-counts, this pass mints a ValueId some op already defines, and
// two values collide in a function nothing about the guard being emitted names. A wrong answer one
// arm's worth of inattention away — the shape the duplication rule exists for.
//
// It also retires a SENTINEL: the id half returned `0` for a non-defining op and was safe only
// because every caller asked the boolean first — and ValueId 0 is a REAL id in this compiler (a
// zero-user-parameter generic method's layout-descriptor slot legitimately holds it, which is why
// `MaxonToStdLowering` carries a separate `hasImplicitLayout` bit rather than testing for 0). The
// union says "no value" in a way no ValueId can spell.
module union DefinedValueLookup
	none
	defines(valueId ValueId)
	// ⭐⭐ **TWO DEFS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS WHY THIS ARM EXISTS — IT CLOSED A LATENT MISCOMPILE.**
	// `tryCall`/`witnessTryCall` write a PRIMARY result AND an ERROR FLAG. Reported by the primary alone —
	// which is what this reader used to do, in as many words — `nextFreeValueId` can return an id that a
	// two-result op already defines, and the next pass to mint from it then defines a value some `tryCall`
	// also defines. Two live values share one id, in a function nothing about the minting site names, with
	// no diagnostic anywhere. `InsertRangeChecks` mints from exactly that number.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHICH OF THE TWO IS HIGHER IS NOT WRITTEN DOWN HERE, AND MUST NOT BE.** One emission site spells
	// `errorFlagId = resultId + 1` (LowerMaxonToStd's builtin-conformance call); the others take both ids
	// from the parser's own mint, from a reserved pairing, or from a `try await` desugar, and nothing holds
	// them in an order. So this reports BOTH and the walk takes the maximum — which is right whichever way
	// round they came, where a rule about their order would be right only until the next producer.
	definesPair(first ValueId, second ValueId)
end 'DefinedValueLookup'

// The `none` set is the terminators / stores / effect ops that define nothing. Exhaustive over StdOp,
// matching `collectStdOpUses`'s discipline — a new variant is a compile error here rather than a
// silently-missed def.
module function stdOpDefinedValue(op StdOp) returns DefinedValueLookup
	return match op 'r'
		const(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		binOp(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		unaryOp(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		cmp(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		binOpImm(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		cmpImm(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		div(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		mod(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		condBranch to branch gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		ret gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		param(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		call(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		retVoid gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		globalAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		loadIndirect(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		storeIndirect gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		rdataAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.definesPair(resultId, second: errorFlagId)
		errorReturn to osExit gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osAllocPages(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// W35: a stream write DEFINES the byte count the OS reports — it used to define nothing, back when
		// its one consumer was a void `print`.
		osWriteStdout(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		funcAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		callIndirect(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// P1.5 subprocess: only `osWaitHandle` (the `WaitForSingleObject` poll) defines a value; the other four
		// consume their `BOOL` via memory or ignore it (see StdDialect's ⭐ note).
		osWaitHandle(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osPanic to osCloseHandle gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// `osFreePages` (P1.5-B1a′) defines nothing; it sits past `osWaitHandle` in the union, so its own arm.
		osFreePages gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// The jump table defines no value — a terminator branches, it does not produce a result.
		switch gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// A witness call DEFINES its result (the dispatched method's return value), like `callIndirect`. The
		// throwing form defines that result AND an error flag, and reports BOTH — see `definesPair`.
		witnessCall(resultId, _, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, _, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.definesPair(resultId, second: errorFlagId)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the FIVE value-defining IOCP ops (iocp/event/thread handles, the read BOOL, the
		// last-error code) capture RAX; osPipeCreate/osLock*/osProcessSpawnInherit define nothing (see StdDialect).
		osPipeCreate gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osIocpCreate(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osEventCreate(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osThreadCreate(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osLockInit to osLockLeave gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osReadOverlapped(resultId, _, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osLastError(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osProcessSpawnInherit gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osCancelIo gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osGetPid(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: osWriteFile captures WriteFile's BOOL (a value); osPipeCreateOutbound defines nothing.
		osPipeCreateOutbound gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osWriteFile(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// The wall-clock read defines nothing — its answer lands in the caller's out-param word.
		osReadWallClock gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// R4.1: the file OPEN captures `CreateFileA`'s HANDLE out of RAX, so unlike the two reads above it
		// DOES define a value — the direct-return shape, not the out-param one.
		osFileOpenRead(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// The close defines NOTHING — `CloseHandle`'s BOOL is ignored, so there is no result to capture.
		osFileClose gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// R4.2. Split exactly as R4.1's pair is: the five ops whose Win32 entry point answers in RAX — a
		// BOOL verdict or a search HANDLE — capture it; `osFileFindClose` discards `FindClose`'s BOOL for
		// the reason `osFileClose` discards `CloseHandle`'s. The 64-bit answers of `osReadFile`/`osFileSize`
		// land in the caller's out-param word, not in a result.
		osReadFile(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileOpenWrite(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osEnvBlock(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osEnvBlockFree gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osHandleFileType(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileSize(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileDelete(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileRename(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileFindFirst(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileFindClose gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// R4.3. All four directory primitives answer in RAX and capture it: `FindNextFileA`/
		// `CreateDirectoryA` a BOOL, `GetCurrentDirectoryA` a character count, `GetFileAttributesA` an
		// attribute mask. None has a void twin, so none takes `none`.
		osFileFindNext(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osDirCreate(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osDirCurrentPath(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileAttributes(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// A3n: the RENDERED panic reads a message pointer; in every other respect it is `osPanic`.
		osPanicValue gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osCommandLine(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// W5. BOTH DEFINE — `GetStdHandle` a HANDLE, `GetModuleFileNameA` a character count. Neither has a
		// void twin, so neither takes `none`, and answering `none` here would not fail to compile: it would
		// under-count `nextFreeValueId` and let this pass mint a live id (see this function's header).
		osStdHandle(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osExecutablePath(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// W35: the stderr write defines the OS's byte count, exactly as its stdout twin above does.
		osWriteStderr(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// A promoted record's address is a def like `globalAddr`'s.
		stackRecordAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// O3: each of the three defines the OS answer it captures from RAX.
		osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osMapNamedSegment(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osTickCountMs(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// The four attached-subprocess host primitives: the two whose Win32 entry point answers in RAX capture
		// it; the two whose BOOL is ignored define nothing, exactly as `osCloseHandle` does.
		osHandleSetInherit gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osPipePeek(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osEnvRead(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osProcessTerminate gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// The eight socket host primitives: the six whose ws2_32 entry point answers in RAX capture it; the two
		// whose answer is discarded define nothing, exactly as `osCloseHandle` does.
		osSocketStartup(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osSocketResolve(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osSocketFreeAddrInfo gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osSocketCreate(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osSocketConnect(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osSocketSend(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osSocketRecv(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osSocketClose gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osCpuCount(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// The thread-CPU read defines nothing — its answer lands in the caller's out-param word, exactly as
		// the wall-clock read's does.
		osThreadCpuTicks gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// F1a: the decommit defines no value. `VirtualFree`'s BOOL is discarded exactly as `osFreePages`
		// discards it — a failed decommit leaves a caller nothing to do.
		osDecommitPages gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// F1b: a fill defines no value — it is a store, and a store answers nothing.
		memFill gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// F1c: both DO define one, and the definition is what makes the failure checkable — see the ops.
		osCommitPages(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osReservePages(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// F2: all three define their result — the CAS its SUCCESS BOOLEAN, the RMW the PRIOR value, the token
		// the identity word. The two atomics carry OPPOSITE conventions; see each op for which and why.
		atomicCas(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		atomicRmw(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		currentThreadToken(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// G1: the two READING ops define their answer (the slot index, the stored word); the two WRITING ones
		// define nothing — `tlsSetValue` publishes into the OS thread's slot and `osSetEvent` into a kernel
		// object, exactly as `osLockEnter`/`osLockLeave` do.
		tlsAlloc(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		tlsSetValue gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		tlsSlotLoad(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osSetEvent gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osEnterBackgroundPriority(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// G3: the ordered load defines the word it read; the ordered store defines nothing — `loadIndirect`
		// and `storeIndirect` exactly.
		loadAcquire(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		storeRelease gives DefinedValueLookup.none
	end 'r'
end 'stdOpDefinedValue'

// Mark every call argument value as used, so a `const` fed ONLY to a call (`f(5)`) is not
// pruned as dead. A `param` has no uses (its result is a def), so it contributes nothing.
function insertUseArgs(referenced StdValueUseSet, args ValueIdArray)
	for arg in args 'each'
		referenced.insert(arg)
	end 'each'
end 'insertUseArgs'

function insertUsePair(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
end 'insertUsePair'

// The three-operand twin of `insertUsePair` — for the subprocess ops that read three value operands
// (`osProcessSpawn`'s cmdline + two out-struct pointers; `osWaitHandles`'s handle array + count + timeout).
function insertUseTriple(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId, c ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
	referenced.insert(c)
end 'insertUseTriple'

// The four-operand twin — for the P1.5 streaming ops that read four value operands (`osPipeCreateOutbound`'s
// name + SA + two out-pointers; `osWriteFile`'s handle + buf + len + bytesOut-pointer).
function insertUseQuad(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId, c ValueId, d ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
	referenced.insert(c)
	referenced.insert(d)
end 'insertUseQuad'

// The five-operand twin — for the P1.5 IOCP ops that read five value operands (`osPipeCreate`'s name + SA + iocp +
// two out-pointers; `osReadOverlapped`'s handle + buf + cap + bytesOut-pointer + overlapped).
function insertUseQuint(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId, c ValueId, d ValueId, e ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
	referenced.insert(c)
	referenced.insert(d)
	referenced.insert(e)
end 'insertUseQuint'

// The six-operand twin — for the two `CreateProcessA` ops, whose command line, working directory,
// creation flags, STARTUPINFO, PROCESS_INFORMATION and environment block are all values.
function insertUseSextet(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId, c ValueId, d ValueId, e ValueId, f ValueId)
	insertUseQuint(referenced, a: a, b: b, c: c, d: d, e: e)
	referenced.insert(f)
end 'insertUseSextet'

// An indirect call reads its callee register AND every argument. The callee is a value operand (the
// code pointer), unlike a direct `call` whose callee is a NAME — so it must be inserted alongside the
// args or the value holding the function pointer would look dead and be pruned.
function insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced StdValueUseSet, calleeReg ValueId, args ValueIdArray)
	referenced.insert(calleeReg)
	insertUseArgs(referenced, args: args)
end 'insertUseCalleeAndArgs'

// `op` with every READ mapped through `subst` — a DENSE column, `subst[v]` being what `v` is
// rewritten to (and `v` itself where nothing changes). The mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: it
// rewrites exactly the operands that one reports, so the two cannot drift. DEFS are left alone
// (a def is not a use), and so are branch-edge args — those live on the predecessor's `IrBlock`,
// not on the op, so rewriting them is `substituteValuesInFunc`'s job — and the reason this is
// file-private where `remappedValue` beside it is not: a pass that reached the op rewrite directly
// could perform half the substitution, which is the failure that function's header names. Its two
// callers are both in THIS file — `substituteValuesInFunc` below, and `remapStdOpForClone`, which
// composes it with the DEF and BLOCK-TARGET halves a clone additionally needs. Exhaustive over StdOp
// with no bare `default`, for the same reason its twin is: a new variant must be a COMPILE ERROR here
// rather than a silently-missed rewrite at every call site.
//
// A COLUMN, NOT A (from, to) PAIR, and that is a performance contract rather than a convenience.
// The pair form could only rewrite ONE value per walk, so a caller with k substitutions to make
// walked the function k times — O(ops x k), and worse, it rebuilt every branch edge k times, which
// made its ALLOCATIONS quadratic too. `elimTrivialBlockArgs` had exactly that shape and it made
// the pass 54% of a large compile. The column form applies all k in a single walk.
function remapStdOpUses(op StdOp, subst ValueIdArray) returns StdOp
	return match op 'r'
		const gives op
		binOp(resultId, lhs, rhs, opcode, operandType) gives StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, operandType) gives StdOp.unaryOp(resultId, operand: remappedValue(operand, subst: subst), opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		cmp(resultId, lhs, rhs, pred, operandType) gives StdOp.cmp(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
		binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode, operandType) gives StdOp.binOpImm(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), imm: imm, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		cmpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, pred, operandType) gives StdOp.cmpImm(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), imm: imm, pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
		div(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) gives StdOp.div(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), signedness: signedness)
		mod(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) gives StdOp.mod(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), signedness: signedness)
		condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) gives StdOp.condBranch(remappedValue(condId, subst: subst), thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId)
		branch gives op
		ret(retValId) gives StdOp.ret(remappedValue(retValId, subst: subst))
		param gives op
		call(resultId, callee, args) gives StdOp.call(resultId, callee: callee, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		retVoid gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s three arms: `globalAddr` reports no use,
		// so it rewrites nothing and is handed back unchanged.
		globalAddr gives op
		loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) gives StdOp.loadIndirect(resultId, addrId: remappedValue(addrId, subst: subst), offset: offset, valueType: valueType)
		storeIndirect(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) gives StdOp.storeIndirect(remappedValue(addrId, subst: subst), offset: offset, valueId: remappedValue(valueId, subst: subst), valueType: valueType)
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm: the size is the one read, so it is the one rewrite.
		osAllocPages(resultId, sizeId) gives StdOp.osAllocPages(resultId, sizeId: remappedValue(sizeId, subst: subst))
		// The mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: both operands are reads, so both are rewritten; `resultId` is a
		// def and is carried through unremapped, exactly as every other defining system op's is.
		osWriteStdout(resultId, bufId, lenId) gives StdOp.osWriteStdout(resultId, bufId: remappedValue(bufId, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(lenId, subst: subst))
		// `rdataAddr` reports no use (its `label` is a name), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back
		// unchanged — the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr` arms.
		rdataAddr gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: `tryCall` rewrites its args (its two results are defs),
		// `errorReturn` rewrites both operands it reads, `osExit` rewrites its code operand.
		tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, callee, args) gives StdOp.tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, callee: callee, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) gives StdOp.errorReturn(remappedValue(retValId, subst: subst), errorFlagId: remappedValue(errorFlagId, subst: subst))
		osExit(codeId) gives StdOp.osExit(remappedValue(codeId, subst: subst))
		// `funcAddr` reports no use (its `funcName` is a name), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back
		// unchanged — the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr`/`funcAddr` arms.
		funcAddr gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: `callIndirect` rewrites its callee register and its args
		// (its result is a def); `resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata carried through unchanged.
		callIndirect(resultId, calleeReg, resultType, argFloatMask, args) gives StdOp.callIndirect(resultId, calleeReg: remappedValue(calleeReg, subst: subst), resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		// `osPanic` reads no value (its `label` is a name), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back
		// unchanged — the mirror of `funcAddr`/`rdataAddr`.
		osPanic gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: `osReadClock` rewrites both out-param addresses it reads,
		// `osSleepMs` rewrites its millisecond count. Both define nothing.
		osReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId) gives StdOp.osReadClock(remappedValue(ticksPtrId, subst: subst), freqPtrId: remappedValue(freqPtrId, subst: subst))
		osSleepMs(msId) gives StdOp.osSleepMs(remappedValue(msId, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s subprocess arms: rewrite exactly the operands they read;
		// `osWaitHandle`'s `resultId` is a def, so it passes through unmapped.
		osProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtr, cwdPtr, creationFlags, startupPtr, procInfoPtr, envPtr) gives StdOp.osProcessSpawn(remappedValue(cmdlinePtr, subst: subst), cwdPtrId: remappedValue(cwdPtr, subst: subst), creationFlagsId: remappedValue(creationFlags, subst: subst), startupPtrId: remappedValue(startupPtr, subst: subst), procInfoPtrId: remappedValue(procInfoPtr, subst: subst), envPtrId: remappedValue(envPtr, subst: subst))
		osProcessExitCode(handle, codePtr) gives StdOp.osProcessExitCode(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), codePtrId: remappedValue(codePtr, subst: subst))
		osWaitHandles(handleArrayPtr, count, timeoutMs) gives StdOp.osWaitHandles(remappedValue(handleArrayPtr, subst: subst), countId: remappedValue(count, subst: subst), timeoutMsId: remappedValue(timeoutMs, subst: subst))
		osCloseHandle(handle) gives StdOp.osCloseHandle(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		osWaitHandle(resultId, handle, timeoutMs) gives StdOp.osWaitHandle(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), timeoutMsId: remappedValue(timeoutMs, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm: the base and the length are the two reads, so both are
		// rewritten — including on x64-windows, where the length then lowers to nothing.
		osFreePages(addr, size) gives StdOp.osFreePages(remappedValue(addr, subst: subst), sizeId: remappedValue(size, subst: subst))
		osPanicValue(messagePtr) gives StdOp.osPanicValue(remappedValue(messagePtr, subst: subst))
		osCommandLine(resultId) gives StdOp.osCommandLine(resultId)
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `switch` arm: only the biased index `subjectId` is a value
		// operand, so it is the one rewrite; the block-id table and default carry through verbatim. Binding
		// the middle-slot Array is safe — a trailing non-Array field (`defaultBlockId`) follows it, exactly
		// the condition that lets `call` bind its trailing args here.
		switch(subjectId, denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId) gives StdOp.switch(remappedValue(subjectId, subst: subst), denseTargetBlockIds: denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId: defaultBlockId)
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `witnessCall` arm: rewrite the witness-table address and the
		// args (its result is a def; `methodIndex`/`resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata carried through).
		witnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) gives StdOp.witnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr: remappedValue(witnessAddr, subst: subst), methodIndex: methodIndex, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s P1.5 IOCP arms: rewrite exactly the value operands they read;
		// the result-only ops (`osIocpCreate`/`osEventCreate`/`osLastError`) rewrite nothing.
		osPipeCreate(namePtr, saPtr, iocp, readPtr, writePtr) gives StdOp.osPipeCreate(remappedValue(namePtr, subst: subst), saPtrId: remappedValue(saPtr, subst: subst), iocpId: remappedValue(iocp, subst: subst), readPtrId: remappedValue(readPtr, subst: subst), writePtrId: remappedValue(writePtr, subst: subst))
		osIocpCreate gives op
		osEventCreate gives op
		osThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtr, arg) gives StdOp.osThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtrId: remappedValue(entryPtr, subst: subst), argId: remappedValue(arg, subst: subst))
		osLockInit(lockPtr) gives StdOp.osLockInit(remappedValue(lockPtr, subst: subst))
		osLockEnter(lockPtr) gives StdOp.osLockEnter(remappedValue(lockPtr, subst: subst))
		osLockLeave(lockPtr) gives StdOp.osLockLeave(remappedValue(lockPtr, subst: subst))
		osReadOverlapped(resultId, handle, buf, cap, bytesOutPtr, ovl) gives StdOp.osReadOverlapped(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), bufId: remappedValue(buf, subst: subst), capId: remappedValue(cap, subst: subst), bytesOutPtrId: remappedValue(bytesOutPtr, subst: subst), ovlId: remappedValue(ovl, subst: subst))
		osLastError gives op
		osProcessSpawnInherit(cmdlinePtr, cwdPtr, creationFlags, startupPtr, procInfoPtr, envPtr) gives StdOp.osProcessSpawnInherit(remappedValue(cmdlinePtr, subst: subst), cwdPtrId: remappedValue(cwdPtr, subst: subst), creationFlagsId: remappedValue(creationFlags, subst: subst), startupPtrId: remappedValue(startupPtr, subst: subst), procInfoPtrId: remappedValue(procInfoPtr, subst: subst), envPtrId: remappedValue(envPtr, subst: subst))
		osCancelIo(handle, ovl) gives StdOp.osCancelIo(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), ovlId: remappedValue(ovl, subst: subst))
		osGetPid gives op
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: the streaming stdin pipe + write, rewriting exactly the value operands they read.
		osPipeCreateOutbound(namePtr, saPtr, childReadPtr, parentWritePtr) gives StdOp.osPipeCreateOutbound(remappedValue(namePtr, subst: subst), saPtrId: remappedValue(saPtr, subst: subst), childReadPtrId: remappedValue(childReadPtr, subst: subst), parentWritePtrId: remappedValue(parentWritePtr, subst: subst))
		osWriteFile(resultId, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) gives StdOp.osWriteFile(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), bufId: remappedValue(buf, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(len, subst: subst), bytesOutPtrId: remappedValue(bytesOutPtr, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm: the out-param address is the one read, so it is the
		// one rewrite. It defines nothing.
		osReadWallClock(ftPtr) gives StdOp.osReadWallClock(remappedValue(ftPtr, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `witnessTryCall` arm: rewrite the witness-table address and the
		// args; both results are defs and every remaining field is metadata carried through.
		witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) gives StdOp.witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, witnessAddr: remappedValue(witnessAddr, subst: subst), methodIndex: methodIndex, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		// R4.1: rewrite the path buffer; `resultId` is a def and is carried through unremapped, exactly as
		// every other defining system op's is.
		osFileOpenRead(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileOpenRead(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileClose(handle) gives StdOp.osFileClose(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		// R4.2: the exact mirrors of `collectStdOpUses`'s seven arms — every value operand it reports read is
		// rewritten here, and every `resultId` is a def carried through unremapped.
		osReadFile(resultId, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) gives StdOp.osReadFile(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), bufId: remappedValue(buf, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(len, subst: subst), bytesOutPtrId: remappedValue(bytesOutPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileOpenWrite(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileOpenWrite(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileOpenWriteExecutable(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osEnvBlock(resultId) gives StdOp.osEnvBlock(resultId)
		osEnvBlockFree(blockPtr) gives StdOp.osEnvBlockFree(remappedValue(blockPtr, subst: subst))
		osHandleFileType(resultId, handle) gives StdOp.osHandleFileType(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		osFileSize(resultId, handle, sizeOutPtr) gives StdOp.osFileSize(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), sizeOutPtrId: remappedValue(sizeOutPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileDelete(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileDelete(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileRename(resultId, oldPathPtr, newPathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileRename(resultId, oldPathPtrId: remappedValue(oldPathPtr, subst: subst), newPathPtrId: remappedValue(newPathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileFindFirst(resultId, pathPtr, findDataPtr) gives StdOp.osFileFindFirst(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst), findDataPtrId: remappedValue(findDataPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileFindClose(handle) gives StdOp.osFileFindClose(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		osFileFindNext(resultId, handle, findDataPtr) gives StdOp.osFileFindNext(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), findDataPtrId: remappedValue(findDataPtr, subst: subst))
		osDirCreate(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osDirCreate(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osDirCurrentPath(resultId, bufLen, bufPtr) gives StdOp.osDirCurrentPath(resultId, bufLenId: remappedValue(bufLen, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileAttributes(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileAttributes(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		// W5. The exact mirrors of `collectStdOpUses`'s two arms: the handle read has no value operand to
		// rewrite, and the path fill rewrites exactly the pair it reads.
		osStdHandle(resultId, which) gives StdOp.osStdHandle(resultId, whichId: remappedValue(which, subst: subst))
		osExecutablePath(resultId, bufLen, bufPtr) gives StdOp.osExecutablePath(resultId, bufLenId: remappedValue(bufLen, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst))
		// W35: `osWriteStdout`'s arm one stream over.
		osWriteStderr(resultId, bufId, lenId) gives StdOp.osWriteStderr(resultId, bufId: remappedValue(bufId, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(lenId, subst: subst))
		// Reports no use (its `slot` is a frame index), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back unchanged —
		// the mirror of `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr`/`funcAddr`.
		stackRecordAddr gives op
		// O3: the two operand-carrying primitives remap theirs; the tick read has none, so it is handed back
		// unchanged like `stackRecordAddr` above.
		osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen) gives StdOp.osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, namePtrId: remappedValue(namePtr, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst), bufLenId: remappedValue(bufLen, subst: subst))
		osMapNamedSegment(resultId, handle, size) gives StdOp.osMapNamedSegment(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), sizeId: remappedValue(size, subst: subst))
		osTickCountMs gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s four attached-subprocess arms: rewrite exactly the operands
		// they read; both results are defs and pass through unmapped.
		osHandleSetInherit(handle) gives StdOp.osHandleSetInherit(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		osPipePeek(resultId, handle, availPtr) gives StdOp.osPipePeek(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), availPtrId: remappedValue(availPtr, subst: subst))
		osEnvRead(resultId, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen) gives StdOp.osEnvRead(resultId, namePtrId: remappedValue(namePtr, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst), bufLenId: remappedValue(bufLen, subst: subst))
		osProcessTerminate(handle, exitCode) gives StdOp.osProcessTerminate(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), exitCodeId: remappedValue(exitCode, subst: subst))
		osSocketStartup(resultId, wsaDataPtr) gives StdOp.osSocketStartup(resultId, wsaDataPtrId: remappedValue(wsaDataPtr, subst: subst))
		osSocketResolve(resultId, hostPtr, hintsPtr, outPtr) gives StdOp.osSocketResolve(resultId, hostPtrId: remappedValue(hostPtr, subst: subst), hintsPtrId: remappedValue(hintsPtr, subst: subst), outPtrId: remappedValue(outPtr, subst: subst))
		osSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtr) gives StdOp.osSocketFreeAddrInfo(remappedValue(addrInfoPtr, subst: subst))
		osSocketCreate(resultId) gives StdOp.osSocketCreate(resultId)
		osSocketConnect(resultId, sock, addrPtr) gives StdOp.osSocketConnect(resultId, sockId: remappedValue(sock, subst: subst), addrPtrId: remappedValue(addrPtr, subst: subst))
		osSocketSend(resultId, sock, bufPtr, len) gives StdOp.osSocketSend(resultId, sockId: remappedValue(sock, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(len, subst: subst))
		osSocketRecv(resultId, sock, bufPtr, len) gives StdOp.osSocketRecv(resultId, sockId: remappedValue(sock, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(len, subst: subst))
		osSocketClose(sock) gives StdOp.osSocketClose(remappedValue(sock, subst: subst))
		// No operands to remap; the def is carried through unchanged, exactly as `osSocketCreate`'s is.
		osCpuCount(resultId) gives StdOp.osCpuCount(resultId)
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm: the out-param address is the one read, so it is the
		// one rewrite. It defines nothing.
		osThreadCpuTicks(outPtr) gives StdOp.osThreadCpuTicks(remappedValue(outPtr, subst: subst))
		// F1a: the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm — base and length are the reads, so both are the
		// rewrites; the op defines nothing to carry through.
		osDecommitPages(addr, size) gives StdOp.osDecommitPages(remappedValue(addr, subst: subst), sizeId: remappedValue(size, subst: subst))
		// F1b: the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm — the destination and the length are the reads, so
		// both are the rewrites, and the constant fill byte rides through untouched.
		memFill(dest, byteCount, value) gives StdOp.memFill(remappedValue(dest, subst: subst), byteCountId: remappedValue(byteCount, subst: subst), value: value)
		// F1c: the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s two arms — the reads are the rewrites, and each result id
		// is a DEF carried through unchanged, exactly as `osAllocPages`'s is.
		osCommitPages(resultId, addr, size) gives StdOp.osCommitPages(resultId, addrId: remappedValue(addr, subst: subst), sizeId: remappedValue(size, subst: subst))
		osReservePages(resultId, sizeId) gives StdOp.osReservePages(resultId, sizeId: remappedValue(sizeId, subst: subst))
		// F2: the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s three arms — every read is a rewrite, each result id is
		// a DEF carried through unchanged, and the RMW's opcode rides along untouched (it names no value).
		atomicCas(resultId, addr, expected, replacement) gives StdOp.atomicCas(resultId, addrId: remappedValue(addr, subst: subst), expectedId: remappedValue(expected, subst: subst), replacementId: remappedValue(replacement, subst: subst))
		atomicRmw(resultId, addr, operand, opcode) gives StdOp.atomicRmw(resultId, addrId: remappedValue(addr, subst: subst), operandId: remappedValue(operand, subst: subst), opcode: opcode)
		currentThreadToken(resultId) gives StdOp.currentThreadToken(resultId)
		// G1: the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s four arms — every read is a rewrite, every result id is
		// a DEF carried through unchanged.
		tlsAlloc(resultId) gives StdOp.tlsAlloc(resultId)
		tlsSetValue(index, value) gives StdOp.tlsSetValue(remappedValue(index, subst: subst), valueId: remappedValue(value, subst: subst))
		tlsSlotLoad(resultId, tebOffset) gives StdOp.tlsSlotLoad(resultId, tebOffsetId: remappedValue(tebOffset, subst: subst))
		osSetEvent(handle) gives StdOp.osSetEvent(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		osEnterBackgroundPriority(resultId) gives StdOp.osEnterBackgroundPriority(resultId)
		// G3: the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s two arms — every read is a rewrite, the load's result
		// id is a DEF carried through unchanged, and neither `offset` nor `valueType` names a value.
		loadAcquire(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) gives StdOp.loadAcquire(resultId, addrId: remappedValue(addrId, subst: subst), offset: offset, valueType: valueType)
		storeRelease(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) gives StdOp.storeRelease(remappedValue(addrId, subst: subst), offset: offset, valueId: remappedValue(valueId, subst: subst), valueType: valueType)
	end 'r'
end 'remapStdOpUses'

// IDENTITY for any id outside the column. The column is sized by the BLOCK-ARG id space (the only
// values a phi-folding pass ever rewrites), and an operand above it — an op result, a parameter —
// is by definition not a block-arg and so is not being rewritten. Same discipline, and the same
// reason, as `pruneDeadBlockArgs.flagOf`.
module function remappedValue(v ValueId, subst ValueIdArray) returns ValueId
	return try subst.get(v) otherwise v
end 'remappedValue'

// Record that every read of `resultId` may read `replacement` instead, extending `subst` to hold the
// id with every NEW slot standing for ITSELF.
//
// ⭐ **THE COLUMN'S BUILDER, BESIDE ITS READER AND ITS APPLIER.** `remappedValue` reads one entry and
// `substituteValuesInFunc` applies the whole column; this is how an entry gets in. It was file-private
// to `foldConstOperands` while that pass was the only builder — `eliminateCommonSubexpressions` is the
// second, and a second copy would be a dense column whose `push`-not-`resize` trap and identity seed
// nothing keeps in step with this one.
//
// ⚠ `resize` cannot build this column and the difference is a miscompile rather than a detail: it
// zero-fills, and a zero in a substitution column means *"rewrite this read to value 0"*. The identity
// seed is what makes `remappedValue` answer "unchanged" for every value the pass does not rewrite — the
// same discipline `elimTrivialBlockArgs` seeds its union-find column with, grown to meet the ids as they
// arrive rather than sized from a bound computed up front.
//
// `push` grows the backing store GEOMETRICALLY, so filling from 0 to the largest rewritten id is linear
// overall — the trap `ConstValueMap.upsert` names, avoided the same way.
//
// The stored value is `replacement` READ THROUGH THE COLUMN, which is what collapses a CHAIN in one
// step — `foldConstOperands`' `(x * 1) + 0` and CSE's third occurrence of an expression whose second
// already pointed at the first. Both callers record in walk order, so a chain's earlier link is always
// already in the column; see `substituteIdentityImmediates` for why that is an optimization rather than
// a correctness requirement.
module function recordValueSubstitution(subst ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, replacement ValueId)
	var next = subst.count()
	while next <= resultId 'grow'
		subst.push(next)
		next = next + 1
	end 'grow'

	try subst.set(resultId, value: remappedValue(replacement, subst: subst)) otherwise panic("recordValueSubstitution: subst.set OOB at {resultId} against a column of {subst.count()}")
end 'recordValueSubstitution'

function remappedArgs(args ValueIdArray, subst ValueIdArray) returns ValueIdArray
	var out = ValueIdArray.create()
	for a in args 'each'
		out.push(remappedValue(a, subst: subst))
	end 'each'
	return out
end 'remappedArgs'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE COPIED OP, REWRITTEN INTO ANOTHER FUNCTION'S COORDINATES — uses, DEFS and BLOCK TARGETS.**
// `inlineLeaves` copies a callee's body into a caller, where every one of those three is a different
// number: a use and a def name the callee's `ValueId`s and a terminator names the callee's `BlockId`s,
// and all three have to move at once or the copy names a mixture of two functions' value spaces.
//
// ⭐ **IT COMPOSES `remapStdOpUses` RATHER THAN RESTATING IT**, so the use half stays where the
// `collectStdOpUses` mirror lives and a new variant is still a compile error in exactly one place. What
// is added here is only what a clone needs beyond a substitution: the DEF fields (which `remapStdOpUses`
// deliberately leaves alone — a def is not a use) and the branch targets.
//
// ⭐ **IT COVERS THE OPS AN INLINABLE LEAF MAY HOLD, AND PANICS ON EVERY OTHER — which is not a gap but
// the eligibility rule's backstop.** `InlineLeaves.scanLeafBody` refuses a callee holding any call,
// any `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` op, or a panic op (whose block is dropped rather than copied), so the
// set that can reach here is closed: the arith band, the two branches, `switch`, the two returns, the
// three address ops and the two indirect accesses. Writing arms for the other seventy would be
// unreachable generality — and a `panic` naming the op is what a disagreement between the two rules
// looks like, instead of a silently mis-cloned body. Every non-clonable variant is covered by a RANGE
// arm ending at the union's last member, so a variant appended past it fails to compile here until it
// is consciously placed.
module function remapStdOpForClone(op StdOp, valueSubst ValueIdArray, blockSubst BlockIdArray) returns StdOp
	let cloned = remapClonedDefsAndTargets(remapStdOpUses(op, subst: valueSubst), valueSubst: valueSubst, blockSubst: blockSubst)
	requireClonedDefAgreesWithTheDefEnumerator(op, cloned: cloned, valueSubst: valueSubst)
	return cloned
end 'remapStdOpForClone'

// ⛔⛔ **THE CHECK BETWEEN TWO PER-VARIANT DEF WALKS — because this file now holds two, and nothing
// else makes them agree.** `stdOpDefinedValue` says WHICH value a variant defines;
// `remapClonedDefsAndTargets` rewrites it. They are exhaustive matches over the same union, so a variant
// APPENDED to `StdOp` is a compile error in both — but an EXISTING arm silently moving to the
// hand-it-back-unchanged side is not: the clone would keep the CALLEE's ValueId, the caller would hold two
// definitions of one value, and the result is a wrong operand rather than a diagnostic. (That is not
// hypothetical for this union: `osWriteStdout` and `osWriteStderr` each GAINED a def in W35, and
// `stdOpDefinedValue`'s own header records `osStdHandle` answering `none` as a defect that "would not fail
// to compile".)
//
// So the def half is verified against the enumerator on every cloned op: whatever `stdOpDefinedValue`
// reports of the original must come back as its image under the column. Two extra jump-table matches per
// copied op, against a splice budget of 24 ops — the price of the two walks not being able to drift apart
// unnoticed.
function requireClonedDefAgreesWithTheDefEnumerator(op StdOp, cloned StdOp, valueSubst ValueIdArray)
	match stdOpDefinedValue(op) 'before'
		none then requireClonedDefines(cloned, expected: DefinedValueLookup.none)
		defines(v) then requireClonedDefines(cloned, expected: DefinedValueLookup.defines(clonedValue(v, subst: valueSubst)))
		// A `tryCall`/`witnessTryCall` is the only two-def shape and `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee holding
		// one, so the cloner never sees it — which is why it has no arm there and none here either.
		definesPair then panic("remapStdOpForClone: a two-def op reached the cloner — only `tryCall` and `witnessTryCall` define a pair, and both are calls, which `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee for")
	end 'before'
end 'requireClonedDefAgreesWithTheDefEnumerator'

function requireClonedDefines(cloned StdOp, expected DefinedValueLookup)
	match stdOpDefinedValue(cloned) 'after'
		none then requireNoDefExpected(cloned, expected: expected)
		defines(actual) then requireDefIs(cloned, actual: actual, expected: expected)
		definesPair then panic("remapStdOpForClone: cloning produced a two-def op from one that is not — the two def walks in this file disagree about `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(cloned)}`")
	end 'after'
end 'requireClonedDefines'

function requireNoDefExpected(cloned StdOp, expected DefinedValueLookup)
	match expected 'want'
		none then break 'want'
		defines(want) then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(cloned)}` DEFINES value {want} by `stdOpDefinedValue` but `remapClonedDefsAndTargets` handed it back with no def rewritten — the clone would keep the callee's id and the caller would hold two definitions of one value")
		definesPair then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(cloned)}` defines a PAIR by `stdOpDefinedValue` and none by the cloner")
	end 'want'
end 'requireNoDefExpected'

function requireDefIs(cloned StdOp, actual ValueId, expected DefinedValueLookup)
	match expected 'want'
		none then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(cloned)}` defines nothing by `stdOpDefinedValue` yet the clone defines value {actual} — the two def walks in this file disagree")
		defines(want) then requireDefEquals(cloned, actual: actual, want: want)
		definesPair then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(cloned)}` defines a PAIR by `stdOpDefinedValue` and one value by the cloner")
	end 'want'
end 'requireDefIs'

function requireDefEquals(cloned StdOp, actual ValueId, want ValueId)
	if actual != want 'defNotRemapped'
		panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(cloned)}` came back defining value {actual} where the splice's column maps the original's def to {want} — the def rewrite and `stdOpDefinedValue` name different fields")
	end 'defNotRemapped'
end 'requireDefEquals'

// The half `remapStdOpUses` does not do: the result ids an op DEFINES, and the block ids a terminator
// names. Its operand fields arrive already rewritten, so they are bound and handed straight back.
function remapClonedDefsAndTargets(op StdOp, valueSubst ValueIdArray, blockSubst BlockIdArray) returns StdOp
	match op 'r'
		const(resultId, value, valueType) then return StdOp.const(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), value: value, valueType: valueType)
		binOp(resultId, lhs, rhs, opcode, operandType) then return StdOp.binOp(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, operandType) then return StdOp.unaryOp(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), operand: operand, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		cmp(resultId, lhs, rhs, pred, operandType) then return StdOp.cmp(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
		binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode, operandType) then return StdOp.binOpImm(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), lhs: lhs, imm: imm, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		cmpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, pred, operandType) then return StdOp.cmpImm(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), lhs: lhs, imm: imm, pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
		div(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then return StdOp.div(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, signedness: signedness)
		mod(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then return StdOp.mod(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, signedness: signedness)
		condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) then return StdOp.condBranch(condId, thenBlockId: clonedBlock(thenBlockId, subst: blockSubst), elseBlockId: clonedBlock(elseBlockId, subst: blockSubst))
		branch(targetBlockId) then return StdOp.branch(clonedBlock(targetBlockId, subst: blockSubst))
		// A return DEFINES nothing and names no block: the splice replaces it with a branch to the
		// continuation rather than cloning it, and it reaches here only through a body op scan.
		ret then return op
		// A `param` is never copied (its value is the call site's argument) and a call of any kind
		// disqualifies the callee, so neither can reach a clone.
		param to call then panic("remapStdOpForClone: a `param`/`call` op reached the cloner — `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee holding a call, and a splice maps a parameter to its argument instead of copying it")
		retVoid then return op
		globalAddr(resultId, label) then return StdOp.globalAddr(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), label: label)
		loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) then return StdOp.loadIndirect(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueType: valueType)
		storeIndirect then return op
		osAllocPages to osWriteStdout then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is marked `isUnsupportedInInlineBody`, which `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee for")
		rdataAddr(resultId, label) then return StdOp.rdataAddr(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), label: label)
		tryCall to osExit then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is a throwing call, a dual-register return or a process exit — each refused by `scanLeafBody`")
		funcAddr(resultId, funcName) then return StdOp.funcAddr(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), funcName: funcName)
		callIndirect then panic("remapStdOpForClone: an indirect call reached the cloner — `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee holding one")
		osPanic to osFreePages then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is a panic or an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` system primitive — a panic BLOCK is redirected to the slow arm rather than copied, and the rest refuse the callee outright")
		switch(subjectId, denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId) then return StdOp.switch(subjectId, denseTargetBlockIds: clonedBlocks(denseTargetBlockIds, subst: blockSubst), defaultBlockId: clonedBlock(defaultBlockId, subst: blockSubst))
		witnessCall to osThreadCpuTicks then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is a witness call or an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` system primitive — both refused by `scanLeafBody`")
		// The appended page/fill/atomic band (F1, F2) and the thread/TLS band (G1), placed consciously: the
		// three `scanLeafBody` ADMITS are cloned like any body op — a fill defines nothing, the two atomics
		// define their result — and the rest are `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` import calls it refuses.
		osDecommitPages then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` page primitive, which `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee for")
		memFill then return op
		osCommitPages to osReservePages then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` page primitive, which `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee for")
		atomicCas(resultId, addrId, expectedId, replacementId) then return StdOp.atomicCas(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), addrId: addrId, expectedId: expectedId, replacementId: replacementId)
		atomicRmw(resultId, addrId, operandId, opcode) then return StdOp.atomicRmw(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), addrId: addrId, operandId: operandId, opcode: opcode)
		currentThreadToken to osEnterBackgroundPriority then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` system primitive, which `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee for")
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is a witness call or an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` system primitive — both refused by `scanLeafBody`")
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is a witness call or an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` system primitive — both refused by `scanLeafBody`")
		// G3: cloned like their plain twins — an ordered load defines its result, an ordered store defines
		// nothing. Inlining copies the op unchanged, which preserves its ordering with it.
		loadAcquire(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) then return StdOp.loadAcquire(clonedValue(resultId, subst: valueSubst), addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueType: valueType)
		storeRelease then return op
		osHandleFileType then panic("remapStdOpForClone: `{stdOpCloneRefusalName(op)}` is an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` system primitive, which `scanLeafBody` refuses a callee for")
	end 'r'
end 'remapClonedDefsAndTargets'

// The refused op's own name, for the panics above — one place, so a message cannot go on naming a
// variant that moved. It reports the two facts that decide admission (`isCall` is not one of them:
// `param` carries it), leaving the reader to look the variant up by them rather than restating the
// union here as a third roster of eighty names.
function stdOpCloneRefusalName(op StdOp) returns String
	return "op in category {op.rawValue.category.name}{" (isUnsupportedInInlineBody)" if op.rawValue.isUnsupportedInInlineBody else ""}"
end 'stdOpCloneRefusalName'

// A cloned op's value, through the splice's dense column. STRICT where `remappedValue` is lenient, and
// the difference is the question: a substitution asks "is this one of the values I am folding?" and
// answers "no" by identity, while a clone asks "what is this callee value called in the caller?" — a
// question with no honest identity answer, because the two functions number their values independently.
// The column covers `0 .. nextFreeValueId(callee)`, so an id outside it is a column built against a
// different function.
//
// ⚠ **`module`-VISIBLE BECAUSE THE SPLICE ITSELF ASKS IT TOO**, of the values no op carries — a copied
// block's phis and its outgoing edge args live on the `IrBlock`, not on any op, so `InlineLeaves` reaches
// them past the cloner. It briefly had its own byte-identical copy under another name, which is one
// column-bounds rule written twice.
module function clonedValue(v ValueId, subst ValueIdArray) returns ValueId
	return try subst.get(v) otherwise panic("remapStdOpForClone: callee value {v} is outside the {subst.count()}-entry value column this splice built — the column is sized by the callee's own `nextFreeValueId`, so an id past it means the op belongs to another function")
end 'clonedValue'

// A cloned terminator's block target, through the splice's dense block column. Every callee block is
// mapped: a copied block to its fresh caller block, a PANIC block to the splice's single slow arm.
function clonedBlock(id BlockId, subst BlockIdArray) returns BlockId
	return try subst.get(id) otherwise panic("remapStdOpForClone: callee block id {id} is outside the {subst.count()}-entry block column this splice built — the column is sized by the callee's own `nextBlockId`")
end 'clonedBlock'

// A dense `switch` table's whole target list, mapped. A jump table names MANY blocks and one default,
// so a clone that reached only the default would leave every case arm branching into the callee.
function clonedBlocks(ids BlockIdArray, subst BlockIdArray) returns BlockIdArray
	var out = BlockIdArray.create()
	for id in ids 'each'
		out.push(clonedBlock(id, subst: subst))
	end 'each'
	return out
end 'clonedBlocks'

// Rewrite every READ in `func` that `subst` maps — in the ops, via `remapStdOpUses` above, and in the
// BRANCH-EDGE args, which live on the predecessor's `IrBlock` rather than on any op and are therefore
// the one half `remapStdOpUses` cannot reach.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE EDGE HALF IS WHY THIS IS ONE FUNCTION AND NOT A CALLER'S TWO LOOPS.** `remapStdOpUses`'s
// own header says edges are "the caller's job", and for a while there was exactly one caller so that
// read as a division of labour. It is not: a pass that rewrites the ops and forgets the edges leaves a
// successor block reading a value the rewrite retired, which is a dangling reference the type checker
// cannot see and the golden shows as a wrong register. TWO passes fold values now
// (`elimTrivialBlockArgs`, `foldConstOperands`), so the pairing lives HERE, beside the three functions
// it drives, and neither pass can perform half of it.
//
// ONE walk, applying ALL of the substitutions — never one walk per substitution. That is a performance
// contract and `remapStdOpUses`' own header carries the measurement behind it (the pair form made
// `elimTrivialBlockArgs` 54% of a large compile).
module function substituteValuesInFunc(module StdModule, func IrFunction, subst ValueIdArray)
	for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("substituteValuesInFunc: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: remapStdOpUses(op, subst: subst)) otherwise panic("substituteValuesInFunc: ops.set({opIdx}) OOB")
	end 'eachOp'

	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachPred'
		var pred = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("substituteValuesInFunc: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		// ⭐ **A BLOCK WHOSE EDGE ARGS THE COLUMN DOES NOT TOUCH KEEPS THE EDGES IT ALREADY HAS.** The
		// rebuild is three allocations per edge — a `BranchEdgeArray`, a `BranchEdge` box and the
		// `ValueIdArray` `remappedArgs` mints — and a substitution typically moves a handful of values in
		// a function with dozens of edges, so almost every one of those rebuilds produced a column
		// identical to the one it replaced. The test below is the same walk over the same args and
		// allocates nothing, so asking first is strictly cheaper than rebuilding blind.
		if not anyEdgeArgIsRemapped(pred, subst: subst) 'edgesUnchanged'
			continue
		end 'edgesUnchanged'

		var newEdges = BranchEdgeArray.create()
		for edge in pred.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
			newEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(edge.targetBlockId, argIds: remappedArgs(edge.argIds, subst: subst)))
		end 'eachEdge'
		pred.branchEdges = newEdges
	end 'eachPred'
end 'substituteValuesInFunc'

// Whether any branch-edge argument of `block` is a value `subst` rewrites — the question that decides
// whether that block's edge column has to be rebuilt at all. It reads exactly the args the rebuild
// rewrites, through the same `remappedValue`, so the two cannot disagree about what moves.
function anyEdgeArgIsRemapped(block IrBlock, subst ValueIdArray) returns bool
	for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
		for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
			if remappedValue(argId, subst: subst) != argId 'moved'
				return true
			end 'moved'
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachEdge'
	return false
end 'anyEdgeArgIsRemapped'

// ⭐⭐ **DELETE EVERY OP THAT DEFINES A VALUE IN `doomed` — THE one block-rebuild both Std passes that
// remove ops share.** `foldConstOperands`' const/identity DCE dooms the defs it detached from their
// readers; `eliminateCommonSubexpressions` dooms the defs it redirected onto an earlier equal
// computation. Each decides its OWN set — the two questions are nothing alike — and neither owns a
// second copy of the walk that acts on it.
//
// It reads the def through `stdOpDefinedValue`, this file's one def enumerator, so a new `StdOp`
// variant is a compile error there rather than an op this walk silently cannot classify.
//
// ⭐ **A BLOCK THAT LOSES NOTHING KEEPS THE COLUMN IT ALREADY HAS.** The rebuild allocates a fresh
// `OpIndexArray` and refills it, and MOST blocks in most functions lose nothing — the unconditional
// rebuild was one allocation per block per function for a column that came out identical. The probe
// below is allocation-free and asks exactly what the rebuild asks, so the two cannot disagree about
// what survives.
//
// ⚠ It removes from `block.opRefs` only. A TERMINATOR is never doomed by either caller (a const and an
// identity `binOpImm` are body ops, and CSE's roster answers `neither` for every control op), and
// removing one would leave a block with no exit at all.
module function removeOpsDefining(module StdModule, func IrFunction, doomed StdValueUseSet)
	if doomed.isEmpty() 'nothingDoomed'
		return
	end 'nothingDoomed'

	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("removeOpsDefining: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		if not blockDefinesADoomedValue(module, block: block, doomed: doomed) 'nothingToRemove'
			continue
		end 'nothingToRemove'

		var kept = OpIndexArray.create()
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachRef'
			if opDefinesADoomedValue(module, opIdx: opIdx, doomed: doomed) 'removed'
				continue
			end 'removed'

			kept.push(opIdx)
		end 'eachRef'
		block.opRefs = kept
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'removeOpsDefining'

// Whether `block` holds any op the removal would drop — the question that decides whether its `opRefs`
// column has to be rebuilt at all.
function blockDefinesADoomedValue(module StdModule, block IrBlock, doomed StdValueUseSet) returns bool
	for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachRef'
		if opDefinesADoomedValue(module, opIdx: opIdx, doomed: doomed) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachRef'
	return false
end 'blockDefinesADoomedValue'

function opDefinesADoomedValue(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, doomed StdValueUseSet) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("removeOpsDefining: opIdx {opIdx} from block.opRefs not in module.ops")
	return match stdOpDefinedValue(op) 'defines'
		none gives false
		defines(valueId) gives doomed.contains(valueId)
		// ⚠ A TWO-DEF op is a `tryCall`/`witnessTryCall` — a CALL, `isPure: false`, which neither caller
		// can doom (a const DCE retires `const`s and identity `binOpImm`s; CSE's roster answers `neither`
		// for every call). It is CHECKED rather than assumed, because dooming ONE half of a pair would
		// delete the op that defines the OTHER, and the survivor's readers would be left naming a value
		// nothing writes.
		definesPair(first, second) gives requireNeitherHalfIsDoomed(doomed, first: first, second: second)
	end 'defines'
end 'opDefinesADoomedValue'

function requireNeitherHalfIsDoomed(doomed StdValueUseSet, first ValueId, second ValueId) returns bool
	if doomed.contains(first) or doomed.contains(second) 'doomedHalfOfAPair'
		panic("removeOpsDefining: value {first}/{second} is one half of a TWO-DEF op (`tryCall`/`witnessTryCall`) and was doomed — removing the op would delete the other def as well, and no caller may doom one")
	end 'doomedHalfOfAPair'

	return false
end 'requireNeitherHalfIsDoomed'

// --- The sugar band contract ---
//
// v1 enforced "no sugar reaches the backend" with a TYPE: sugar ops existed in
// `StdOp` and simply had no `MirOp` counterpart, so the Std→MIR lowering could not
// represent them past the boundary. Collapsing the tier gives that up, and the
// replacement is deliberate rather than accidental — two independent guards:
//
//   1. Each sugar variant gets an explicit `panic("… must be desugared by
//      lowerToMachineForm")` arm in the backend's `match` — NOT a bare `default`,
//      which the project forbids precisely because it swallows new cases.
//   2. `assertNoSugarOps` below, a whole-module gate. It catches an op the
//      per-variant arms would miss — one reachable only through a `match` range arm,
//      or sitting in a block the backend's own walk happens not to visit.
//
// Both are vacuous at M1 (no sugar variants exist yet), so `lowerToMachineForm`
// itself is NOT written now. It arrives with the first sugar op (M6's `drop`/`free`,
// M14's descriptor/witness ops), as a Std→Std pass.
//
// The gate is run by `buildBackend` — the single TARGET-NEUTRAL backend entry — not
// inside `lowerStdToX64`. Sugar is a property of the Std tier, not of x64, so every
// future backend (arm64, wasm) inherits the check instead of having to remember to
// re-install it.
//
// It walks LIVE ops via `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, NOT the flat `module.ops` array:
// `lowerToMachineForm` is a block-rebuilding pass, so the sugar ops it desugars
// survive in `module.ops` as orphans. Scanning the flat array would panic on those
// dead entries and report a desugaring failure that never happened.
export function assertNoSugarOps(module StdModule)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("assertNoSugarOps: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			if op.rawValue.category == StdOpCategory.sugar 'isSugar'
				panic("Std→Target: a `sugar`-category StdOp survived into the backend in function '{String.from(func.name)}' — lowerToMachineForm did not run, or does not handle this op")
			end 'isSugar'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'assertNoSugarOps'

// The block ids control can transfer to from `block` — its terminator's targets, appended to `out`.
//
// ⭐ **THREE ARMS NAME A SUCCESSOR AND EVERY OTHER OP NAMES NONE, WHICH IS AN ANSWER RATHER THAN A
// DEFAULT.** An op that is not a branch either LEAVES THE FUNCTION (`ret`, `retVoid`, `errorReturn`) or
// leaves the PROCESS (`osExit`, `osPanic`, `osPanicValue`) — in both cases control reaches no other
// block of this function — or it is a body op that cannot be a terminator at all, which is
// `IrModule.setTerminator`'s contract to keep and not this walk's to re-check. The question asked here
// is "where can control go from here", and for every one of them the answer is "nowhere else".
//
// The Target tier's twin is `TargetLiveness.collectBlockSuccessorIds`, and the two cannot be shared: a
// Target block's second successor is a `jcc` BODY op (`block.condBranch`), while a Std block's whole
// exit is its one terminator.
//
// ⭐ It lives HERE, beside `collectStdOpUses` and `stdOpDefinedValue`, because it is the third
// exhaustive `StdOp` roster and they belong in one file: a new variant is then a compile error in three
// adjacent matches rather than in three files that learn about it separately. TWO readers —
// `foldConstants.dropUnreachableBlocks`, which owns the reachability walk this used to live beside, and
// `StdDominatorTree.build`, which needs the same edges to compute dominance.
module function collectStdBlockSuccessorIds(module StdModule, block IrBlock, out BlockIdArray)
	if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerminatorOp'
		return
	end 'noTerminatorOp'

	let op = try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("collectStdBlockSuccessorIds: the terminator of block {block.id} is not in module.ops")
	match op 'term'
		const to mod then break 'term'
		condBranch(_, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) then pushBothTargets(out, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId)
		branch(targetBlockId) then out.push(targetBlockId)
		ret to osFreePages then break 'term'
		switch(_, denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId) then pushSwitchTargets(out, denseTargetBlockIds: denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId: defaultBlockId)
		witnessCall to osEnterBackgroundPriority then break 'term'
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then break 'term'
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then break 'term'
		loadAcquire or storeRelease then break 'term'
		osHandleFileType then break 'term'
	end 'term'
end 'collectStdBlockSuccessorIds'

// A conditional branch's two targets. A helper because a `match` arm is ONE statement and both edges
// have to be recorded — the same shape `insertUsePair` takes for a two-operand op.
function pushBothTargets(out BlockIdArray, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId)
	out.push(thenBlockId)
	out.push(elseBlockId)
end 'pushBothTargets'

// A jump table's targets: every dense arm plus the default. Duplicates cost nothing — a reachability
// walk marks a position once however many edges name it, and a CSR adjacency simply lists the edge
// twice.
function pushSwitchTargets(out BlockIdArray, denseTargetBlockIds BlockIdArray, defaultBlockId BlockId)
	for targetBlockId in denseTargetBlockIds 'eachArm'
		out.push(targetBlockId)
	end 'eachArm'

	out.push(defaultBlockId)
end 'pushSwitchTargets'
